  - Approach: Hybrid capture of incompletely spliced, chromatin-associated pre-mRNAs; deep sequencing after transiently freezing transcription; deep learning with feature attribution.
  - Findings: Many introns in NF-κB–responsive genes splice 10–100× slower than average. Conserved introns with weak donor sites act as timing elements; strengthening donor sites by as few as two bases in minigene assays markedly increases expression. Deep learning identified additional intronic/exonic cis-elements (including non-canonical motifs) that contribute to delayed splicing independent of donor strength.
  - Implications: Temporal control of pre-mRNA splicing modulates onset and amplitude of inflammatory gene expression; similar timing mechanisms may operate in other rapid-response programs.

- Ddx3x regulates B-cell development and immunoglobulin rearrangement in mice (doi:10.1101/452086)
  - Approach: Conditional deletion (Mb1-Cre, Vav1-Cre) in mice; analyses of development, cell cycle, repertoire.
  - Findings: Female Mb1-Cre Ddx3x−/−: complete loss of mature peripheral B cells due to a pro-B block. Male hemizygous deletion: milder reductions with marginal zone skewing, suggesting partial compensation by Ddx3y/other male factors. Mechanism: reduced Brwd1, failure to curtail proliferation, defective Igκ rearrangement; repertoire skewed to λ light chain.
  - Implications: Ddx3x is essential for coordinating proliferative and epigenetic programs enabling immunoglobulin gene rearrangement and normal B-cell development.

