Tool `Paper Search:search_pubmed` with Parameter {'query': 'melanoma BRAF review 2024 2025', 'max_results': 20} on Paper Search succeeded. Result (summarized from 5103 tokens): - Development and Evaluation of a Web-Based Outcome Database for Advanced Melanoma with Rare BRAF Mutations (Dugas-Breit et al., 2025): Describes creation and assessment of a web-based registry to capture outcomes for advanced melanoma with rare BRAF mutations, addressing a significant evidence gap in this understudied subgroup.

- Advances in the diagnosis and treatment of primary malignant melanoma of the vagina (Lu et al., 2025): Primary vaginal melanoma is rare (0.3–0.8% of female melanomas) and highly aggressive with very poor prognosis; presents a case and systematic review highlighting diagnostic and therapeutic challenges.

- Personalized therapies in advanced BRAFV600-mutated melanoma: 3 REMINISCENCE case reports (Gebhardt et al., 2025): In patients with brain metastases, sequencing of immune checkpoint inhibitors (ICI) and targeted therapy (TT; encorafenib/binimetinib) remains contentious, especially post-adjuvant therapy. Trial data (DREAMseq, SECOMBIT, EBIN, ImmunoCobiVem) may not generalize due to prior-treatment-altered tumor microenvironments and design differences. Early circulating tumor DNA (ctDNA)-guided strategies are promising; calls for prospective biomarker-driven trials.

- Cutaneous Melanoma: A Review (Joshi et al., 2025): US melanoma incidence rose from 8.8/100,000 (1975) to 28.42/100,000 (2022); cutaneous melanoma accounts for 94% of cases with 104,960 US cases projected for 2025. Summarizes epidemiology and contemporary management.

- Effects of antioxidants on cancer progression (Schmidt et al., 2025): Antioxidant effects are context-dependent. While some slow tumor growth (e.g., MYC-driven lymphoma), others can accelerate tumor progression, metastasis, and angiogenesis (including BRAF-driven melanoma and KRAS-driven lung cancer). Some antioxidants may enhance responses to ICI. Emphasizes caution with supplementation in oncology.

- Management of metastatic uveal melanoma: French expert consensus guidelines (Rodrigues et al., 2025): Metastatic uveal melanoma (20–30% risk; liver-predominant) has poor prognosis and distinct genomics (typically lacking BRAF/NRAS/KIT). Recommends comprehensive workup (liver biopsy, imaging, ctDNA, high-definition HLA typing for HLA-A*02:01). Tebentafusp is standard for HLA-A*02:01-positive patients. Local liver-directed therapies (surgery, isolated hepatic perfusion, chemoembolization) for limited hepatic disease; for HLA-A*02:01-negative extensive disease, clinical trials preferred; alternatives include percutaneous hepatic perfusion or ICI (alone/combination). Assess response with RECIST, tumor growth rate, and ctDNA dynamics.

- Evolving treatment paradigms for melanoma brain metastases (Grant et al., 2025): Up to 60% of advanced melanoma patients develop brain metastases; prognosis remains poor. Reviews ICIs, BRAF/MEK inhibitors, stereotactic radiosurgery (SRS), and combinations; optimal integration and sequencing remain unresolved.

- Advances in personalized medicine for cancer (Jamalinia & Weiskirchen, 2025): Genomic profiling enables precision targeted therapies that improve efficacy, reduce adverse effects, and address tumor heterogeneity, underscoring the broader oncology shift toward individualized care.

- Cellular Therapy for Advanced Melanoma (Zaemes & Gibney, 2025): Lifileucil (TIL therapy) received FDA approval in 2024 for metastatic melanoma after anti-PD-1 and BRAF inhibitor (V600) treatment. Ongoing development seeks safer, more effective TIL strategies. Additional cellular approaches (TCR-transduced T cells, T-cell engagers) show anti-tumor activity.

- Neoadjuvant Therapy in Melanoma (Farooq et al., 2025): Neoadjuvant therapy shows advantages over adjuvant therapy, including stronger antitumor immunity, improved prognostication, and enabling personalized surgical/medical decisions based on response; practice is shifting accordingly, with ongoing trials to refine strategies.

