- Paper ID: 40341941
  - Title: Targeting the MAPK pathway for NRAS mutant melanoma: from mechanism to clinic.
  - Authors: Wang Y; Xu G; Xia H
  - DOI: 10.1093/bjd/ljaf178
  - Published: 2025-01-01
  - Source/URL: PubMed — https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/40341941/
  - Key points:
    - NRAS is the second most common mutation in melanoma; prognosis is poor and effective targeted options remain limited.
    - Oncogenic mechanism: Mutant NRAS activates RAF-MEK-ERK signaling to drive melanoma.
    - Therapeutic advances:
      - MEK inhibitor tunlametinib.
      - Combination therapy: pan-RAF inhibitor naporafenib plus MEK inhibitor trametinib.
    - Scope of review: Direct targeting of mutant NRAS, downstream RAF and MEK inhibition, and combinations with other modalities (including immunotherapy) to treat NRAS-mutant melanoma.
    - Discusses current challenges in targeted therapy for NRAS-mutant melanoma and emerging countermeasures.

- Paper ID: 39577552
  - Title: A comprehensive review of targeting RAF kinase in cancer.
  - Author: Hossain MA
  - DOI: 10.1016/j.ejphar.2024.177142
  - Published: 2025-01-01
  - Source/URL: PubMed — https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/39577552/
  - Key points:
    - RAF kinases, especially BRAF, are central to the MAPK/ERK pathway governing proliferation, differentiation, and survival; dysregulation via BRAF mutations or upstream alterations (e.g., Ras, RTKs) drives oncogenesis.
    - BRAF V600E is prevalent across malignancies (highest in melanoma).
    - Clinical impact of RAF-targeted therapies:
      - Marked efficacy in BRAF V600E–mutant melanoma with inhibitors such as vemurafenib and dabrafenib, improving outcomes.
      - Benefits observed in some thyroid and colorectal cancers, though generally less pronounced than in melanoma.
    - Resistance challenges:
      - Mechanisms include RAF dimerization, feedback reactivation of MAPK signaling, and paradoxical ERK activation.
      - Consequences: reduced efficacy over time, disease progression, and possible secondary malignancies.
    - Strategies to overcome resistance:
      - Combination approaches (e.g., RAF plus MEK inhibitors).
      - Development of next-generation RAF inhibitors to improve specificity and counter resistance.
    - Emphasis on ongoing clinical and translational efforts to enhance durability and breadth of responses in RAF-targeted oncology.

Cross-cutting themes:
- The MAPK/ERK pathway remains a central therapeutic axis across cancers with NRAS/BRAF alterations.
- While targeted inhibition (RAF/MEK) has yielded major advances, resistance and context-dependent adverse effects (e.g., potential pro-metastatic signaling upon BRAF inhibition in certain models) are critical considerations.
- Combination regimens and next-generation inhibitors are leading strategies to improve outcomes and mitigate resistance.
