1) Evaluating large language models for predicting psychiatric acute readmissions (PsyRoBERTa)
- Aim: Build and evaluate a psychiatry-specialized LLM trained on non-English, population-based EHR notes to predict psychiatric acute readmissions.
- Data/Model: ~44 million psychiatric-relevant clinical notes from eastern Denmark; PsyRoBERTa compared to three public models (general/medical domain) and a logistic regression baseline.
- Results: PsyRoBERTa outperformed comparators for readmission prediction (AUC=0.74) and recognized psychiatric diagnoses (AUC=0.85). Captured clinically aligned features.
- Implications: Domain-specific pretraining materially improves performance on psychiatric clinical notes; enables leveraging text for psychiatric outcome prediction.

2) Inferring pathogen superspreading potential from early spatial spread
- Aim: Estimate superspreading (dispersion parameter r) from early spatial patterns rather than cluster sizes/genomics.
- Methods: Branching process model over US inter-county mobility; secondary infections ~ negative binomial (mean R0, dispersion r). Graph neural network infers r from early spread. Robustness to R0 variability and underreporting tested.
- Findings: Stronger superspreading slows early spatial invasion and increases realization variability. Applied to early US COVID-19: strong superspreading pre-lockdown (r=0.50, 95% CI 0.23–1.20), weaker after (r=1.3, 0.64–3.18).
- Implications: Population-level spatial data can quantify superspreading potential early, aiding control strategy design.

3) Later trading hours and alcohol-related harms in Scotland
- Setting: Natural experiment; 2015–2021 controlled interrupted time series.
- Policy: Aberdeen—38 bars extended 1–3 hours up to 3am; Glasgow—10 nightclubs extended 1 hour to 4am. Control: Edinburgh.
- Outcomes: Weekend nighttime alcohol-related ambulance call-outs (primary); crimes (secondary).
- Results: Aberdeen saw a 17.4% relative increase in ambulance call-outs (effect size 7.061; 95% CI 3.902–10.220; p<0.001) and a 7.9% rise in crimes (effect size 3.187; 95% CI 0.564–5.810; p=0.017; not robust in sensitivity analyses). No significant, robust effects in Glasgow.
- Implications: Later hours increased harms where more/larger premises extended; effects likely moderated by number, capacity, type of premises; important for licensing policy design.

4) Sex differences in protective effect of brain volume after ischemic stroke
- Cohort: 1,103 AIS patients (48% female), 2015–2024 multicenter registry.
- Measures: Parenchymal brain volume (PBV, non-contrast CT), final infarct volume (DWI-FIV, 24–48h MRI), NIHSS; outcome 90-day mRS 0–2.
- Results: Functional independence: 51.7% males vs 44.9% females (p=0.025). PBV protective in both sexes univariably; multivariably, effect persisted in males (aOR 1.30 per 100 cm3; 95% CI 1.10–1.60) but not females (aOR 1.10; 95% CI 0.90–1.33). Each decade reduced independence odds by 30% in females (aOR 0.70; 0.62–0.79) vs 15% in males (aOR 0.85; 0.72–0.97; interaction p=0.019). Age associated with larger infarcts in females (+4.22 cm3/decade; p=0.033), not males.
- Implications: Aging attenuates brain reserve benefits more in women; incorporate sex and PBV into outcome models.

5) Concordance with IDSA criteria for UTI testing/treatment in the ED
- Design: 15-month review of 50 adult ED patients with urinalysis (UA); excluded sepsis/needs for empiric antibiotics. Aim: assess adherence to IDSA guidance (avoid testing/treating ASB except pregnant and select procedures).
