- Elevated SIGLEC8 in cutaneous melanoma (2025, Li et al.)
  - Finding: Higher SIGLEC8 expression in skin cutaneous melanoma (SKCM) predicts better prognosis.
  - Mechanistic link: Associated with hematopoietic cell lineage pathway.
  - Context: Traditional prognostic markers (TNM, BRAF status, TMB) have limitations; SIGLEC8 may reflect tumor microenvironment–immune regulation relevance.

- Melanoma “3.0T”: technology and therapy innovations (2025, Lawrence et al.)
  - Early detection: Teledermatology and AI-supported mobile apps may improve access and reduce disparities, but real-world performance of current AI models drops; focus shifting to AI that augments clinician decisions and improves outcomes.
  - Targeted therapy: BRAF/MEK inhibitors established for BRAF V600; acquired resistance remains a key challenge.
  - Beyond V600: Novel agents/combinations show promise for NRAS, CDKN2A, and class II/III BRAF mutations.
  - Immunotherapy gaps: ≥50% with metastatic melanoma do not achieve durable benefit from ICIs.
  - Cell therapy: Tumor-infiltrating lymphocytes (TILs) can yield durable responses in a minority; newer T-cell therapies under evaluation.

- Facts and hopes in melanoma (2025, Flaherty et al.)
  - Outcomes: Stage IV melanoma death rates have fallen 3–5% annually over the past decade due to targeted therapy and immunotherapy advances.
  - Approvals/strategies: BRAF/MEK inhibitors; checkpoint inhibitors targeting CTLA-4, PD-1, LAG-3; gp100 T-cell engager; TIL therapy.
  - Early-stage: Adjuvant and neoadjuvant therapies reduce relapse in stage II–III.
  - Unmet needs: Refractory/recurrent disease and rare subtypes (mucosal, acral, uveal).
  - Future directions: Engineered T cells (TCR, CAR-T), CRISPR editing, deeper understanding of tumor microenvironment, heterogeneity, and the microbiome.

- MIF family and CD74 in melanoma (2024, Tanese & Ogata)
  - Role: MIF and its receptor CD74 influence melanoma biology, aiding immune evasion and treatment resistance.
  - Implication: CD74/MIF axis is a potential therapeutic target; review details cellular/molecular functions and tumor roles.

- BRAF inhibitors: 2002–2022 challenges and opportunities (2023, Singh et al.)
  - Overview: BRAF is central to MAPK signaling; three BRAF mutation classes identified (I: V600 monomers; II: RAS-independent dimers; III: kinase-impaired, RAS-dependent).
  - Context: Two decades of drug development, resistance mechanisms, and class-specific therapeutic strategies discussed.

- Ferroptosis as a therapeutic avenue (2023, Manzari Tavakoli et al.)
  - Concept: Ferroptosis is an iron-dependent cell death distinct from apoptosis/necrosis; relevant to melanoma.
  - Therapeutics: Ferroptosis inducers, MEK/BRAF inhibitors, miR-137/miR-9, and MHC II–targeting strategies show potential.
  - Strategy: Combining ferroptosis induction with targeted therapy or ICIs may enhance response rates and overcome resistance.

- Non-V600 BRAF-mutant cancers and MAPK-targeted therapies (2022, Dankner et al.)
  - Epidemiology: Non-V600 BRAF mutations ≈35% of all BRAF mutations; many are oncogenic.
  - Classification: Class 2 (RAS-independent dimers) and class 3 (kinase-impaired, RAS-dependent).
  - Clinical gap: No consensus treatment strategies yet for class 2/3; emerging targeted approaches under evaluation.

- Case report: BRAF L485–P490 deletion (2022, Zhang et al.)
  - Finding: Metastatic melanoma with BRAF L485–P490 deletion responded to combined BRAF and MEK inhibition.
  - Implication: Some non-V600 alterations may be sensitive to MAPK pathway blockade.

- Triplet therapy (BRAF+MEK+PD-1/PD-L1) in BRAF-mutant melanoma (2021, Trojaniello et al.)
  - Rationale: BRAF/MEK gives high but often short-lived responses; ICIs yield fewer but more durable responses.
  - Hypothesis: Triplet regimens could overcome each class’s limitations and prolong benefit; supported by preclinical/translational data
