Trends and outcomes
- Advanced melanoma outcomes have markedly improved over the past decade, with stage IV death rates decreasing 3–5% annually, driven by targeted therapy and immunotherapy advances.
- Durable long-term responses now occur in up to half of patients with advanced disease; however, substantial unmet needs persist for recurrent, nonresponsive disease and rarer subtypes (mucosal, acral, uveal).

Early detection and technology
- Teledermatology and AI-assisted mobile skin screening apps may help close disparities in early detection and access to specialty care.
- Despite rapid growth of AI in dermatology, prospective validation on real-world, multimodal datasets shows performance drops, limiting routine clinical use. Current focus: AI that augments clinician decision-making and demonstrably improves patient outcomes.

Current systemic therapy landscape
- Two dominant classes:
  - Immune checkpoint inhibitors (ICIs): target CTLA-4, PD-1, and more recently LAG-3; provide lower initial response rates than targeted therapy but more durable benefits.
  - Targeted therapy: BRAF and MEK inhibitors for BRAF V600-mutant melanoma (~50% of cases) are established in both adjuvant and metastatic settings.
- Additional modalities:
  - T-cell engager therapy targeting gp100 has first-in-class approvals.
  - Tumor-infiltrating lymphocyte (TIL) therapy offers durable responses in a subset of ICI-refractory patients; newer T-cell therapies (TCR-engineered cells, CAR-T, CRISPR-edited approaches) are in trials.

Targeted therapy details and resistance
- BRAF biology:
  - Class I: V600 mutants, RAS-independent active monomers.
  - Class II: RAS-independent activated dimers.
  - Class III: kinase-impaired; signal via increased RAS binding and CRAF activation.
- Clinical implications: FDA-approved BRAF/MEK combinations (e.g., dabrafenib/trametinib; encorafenib/binimetinib) benefit V600-mutant disease; acquired resistance remains a key challenge.
- Non-V600 mutations (class II/III), NRAS, CDKN2A alterations: emerging agents and combinations show promise.
- Triplet strategies (BRAF + MEK + PD-1/PD-L1 blockade) aim to combine high response rates with durability; supported by preclinical/early translational rationale and ongoing clinical evaluation.

Immunotherapy advances and limitations
- ICIs against CTLA-4, PD-1, and LAG-3 have transformed care; yet ≥50% of metastatic patients do not achieve long-term benefit.
- Evolving strategies include novel ICI combinations and cellular therapies to overcome resistance and broaden durable responses.

Sequencing, adjuvant, and neoadjuvant therapy (key 2022–2023 updates)
- First-line sequencing: initiating therapy with ICIs improves survival compared with starting with BRAF/MEK inhibitors in metastatic BRAF-mutant melanoma.
- Adjuvant therapy:
  - Established benefit of anti-PD-1 and BRAF/MEK inhibitors in resected stage III disease, with 5-year data informing durability.
  - Expanding to resected stage IIB/IIC patients, who can have risks comparable to some stage III cases; new data support benefit in this group.
- Neoadjuvant therapy: a practice-changing trial showed improved outcomes for patients with macroscopic, surgically resectable disease using a neoadjuvant approach.

Prevention, epidemiology, and biology
- Melanoma incidence continues to rise, with a shift toward earlier-stage diagnoses.
- UV radiation drives carcinogenesis via DNA photoproducts and reactive oxygen species; prevention strategies include UV avoidance and investigation of antioxidant agents.
- BRAF mutation prevalence varies by subtype: ~59% in intermittently sun-exposed cutaneous sites (trunk/arms), lower in acral (~23%), mucosal (~11%), and absent in uveal melanoma.

Historical milestones and pivotal trials
- 2010–2011: Ipilimumab (anti-CTLA-4) and vemurafenib (BRAFV600E inhibitor) demonstrated survival benefits, inaugurating the modern era.
- Vemurafenib (B
