Second-line cholangiocarcinoma (CCA)
- Conventional chemotherapy has limited benefit post–first line. Precision strategies are advancing, driven by biomarkers: IDH mutations, BRAF alterations, NTRK fusions, FGFR2 fusions, and HER2/neu overexpression.
- Targeted and immunotherapy agents—alone or combined with chemo/IO—are under evaluation in trials; optimizing selection and personalization aims to extend survival and quality of life.

Melanoma precision medicine: current and emerging
- Standard of care: BRAF mutation testing to guide BRAF/MEK inhibitors; major survival gains in metastatic cutaneous melanoma.
- Less common targets actionable in subsets: NTRK fusions, KIT mutations. Acral and mucosal melanomas (more prevalent in East Asians) are often less responsive to ICI and BRAF/MEK therapy; comprehensive genomic profiling remains essential, with regional data gaps (e.g., Japan).
- Diagnostics/monitoring: gene expression profiling assays and nomograms for prognostication and SLNB risk; circulating tumor DNA for response tracking—available but not broadly guideline-endorsed. TIL therapy limited to specialized centers; personalized mRNA vaccines remain investigational but promising.
- Uveal melanoma: HLA-A*02:01 required for tebentafusp.
- Predictive ICI biomarkers (beyond MSI-H/TMB-high) remain unvalidated in many guidelines.

Spitz tumors and spitzoid melanoma: classification, drivers, and prognosis
- Spectrum: benign Spitz nevus (SN), intermediate atypical Spitz tumor (AST), malignant Spitz melanoma (SM). Histology overlaps melanoma; diagnosis benefits from expert review plus immunohistochemistry and genomics.
- WHO conceptualization: Spitz tumors defined by MAPK-activating initiating events, predominantly kinase fusions; benign, intermediate, and malignant lesions exist within each molecular class. Some melanomas mimic Spitz morphology but harbor conventional BRAF/NRAS drivers (“spitzoid melanoma”), distinct from fusion-driven Spitz neoplasms.
- Canonical drivers (generally mutually exclusive): RTK fusions (ROS1, ALK, NTRK1/2/3, RET, MET), serine/threonine kinase fusions/mutations (BRAF, MAP3K8, MAP2K1), HRAS mutations (±11p amplification), and rarer aberrations. MAP3K8 fusions, although uncommon, are enriched in more atypical/malignant lesions.
