Tool `Paper Search:search_pubmed` with Parameter {'query': 'MK-8353 melanoma ERK inhibitor', 'max_results': 20} on Paper Search succeeded. Result (summarized from 1210 tokens): - Biology and signaling:
  - ERK1/2 are central kinases in the Ras–Raf–MEK–ERK MAPK cascade, regulating apoptosis, proliferation, immune and nervous system functions, and RNA synthesis/processing.
  - Activation: MEK1/2 phosphorylate ERK1/2 within the activation segment—first Y204 (ERK1)/Y187 (ERK2), then T202 (ERK1)/T185 (ERK2); both events are required for activity.
  - Substrate specificity: Raf kinases phosphorylate only MEK1/2; MEK1/2 phosphorylate only ERK1/2; ERK1/2 phosphorylate numerous cytoplasmic and nuclear targets (e.g., transcription factors, regulators).
  - ERK1/2 are proline-directed kinases preferring PxS/TP motifs; the MAPK pathway branches extensively at the ERK node.
  - Inactivation via dual-specificity phosphatases, protein tyrosine phosphatases, and serine/threonine phosphatases; kinase/phosphatase interplay makes signaling reversible. Cell cycle progression involves tightly timed >32,000 phosphorylation/dephosphorylation events (mass spectrometry).

- Oncologic relevance and current therapies:
  - The MAPK cascade is a major oncogenic driver. Targeted inhibitors are a key antitumor strategy, with current approved oral agents in this module limited to BRAF-mutant melanoma.
  - Most effective regimens combine a BRAF inhibitor with a MEK inhibitor: dabrafenib + trametinib, encorafenib + binimetinib, vemurafenib + cobimetinib.
  - Resistance to BRAF/MEK inhibitors typically emerges within ~1 year; additional tumor types likely are amenable to MAPK-pathway inhibition.

- Rationale for ERK inhibition and agents:
  - Constitutive ERK1/2 activation is common in cancer; ERK reactivation is a well-known resistance mechanism to upstream MAPK inhibitors.
  - ERK inhibitors may overcome resistance. Ulixertinib, MK-8353, and GDC-0994 are orally active, potent, selective ERK1/2 inhibitors in early clinical trials for advanced/metastatic solid tumors.
  - These ERK inhibitors show activity in cell lines resistant to BRAF and MEK inhibitors.

- MK-8353 and dual-mechanism ERK inhibition:
  - MK-8353, an oral ERK1/2 inhibitor under clinical development for advanced solid tumors, does not directly inhibit MEK1/2 but decreases ERK1/2 phosphorylation and phosphorylation of the ERK substrate RSK.
  - Mechanism: ERK catalytic inhibition lowers RSK phosphorylation; binding of MK-8353 to ERK impedes MEK from phosphorylating ERK, reducing p-ERK—an “ERK dual mechanism inhibition” paradigm.
  - The preclinical dual-mechanism ERK inhibitor SCH772984 showed promising activity across BRAFV600- and RAS-mutant cancer cell lines and xenografts, supporting the clinical advancement of MK-8353.

- Outlook:
  - Further clinical studies are needed to determine whether ERK inhibitors can deliver durable benefit and delay or prevent MAPK-pathway resistance.

- Sources:
  - Roskoski R. Targeting ERK1/2 protein-serine/threonine kinases in human cancers. Pharmacol Res. 2019; doi:10.1016/j.phrs.2019.01.039. https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/30794926/
  - Moschos SJ et al. Development of MK-8353, an orally administered ERK1/2 inhibitor, in patients with advanced solid tumors. JCI Insight. 2018; doi:10.1172/jci.insight.92352. https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/29467321/
