Tool `Paper Search:search_pubmed` with Parameter {'query': 'melanoma BRAF review 2024', 'max_results': 20} on Paper Search succeeded. Result (summarized from 5023 tokens): - Landscape and epidemiology
  - Cutaneous melanoma accounts for 94% of melanoma cases in the US; incidence rose from 8.8 per 100,000 (1975) to 28.42 per 100,000 (2022), with 104,960 US cases projected for 2025.
  - Melanoma brain metastases (MBM) occur in up to 60% of advanced cases and carry high neurologic morbidity and poor prognosis.
  - Uveal melanoma (UM) metastasizes in 20–30% of patients—predominantly to the liver—with poor overall survival; UM lacks common cutaneous driver mutations (BRAF/NRAS/KIT), limiting targeted therapy options.

- Targeted therapy, immunotherapy, and sequencing
  - For BRAFV600-mutated melanoma, optimal sequencing of targeted therapy (BRAF/MEK) and immune checkpoint inhibitors (ICI) remains unsettled. Case-based insights (REMINISCENCE project with encorafenib/binimetinib in patients with brain metastases) highlight:
    - Prior therapies can alter the tumor microenvironment, limiting generalizability of trial data (DREAMseq, SECOMBIT, EBIN, ImmunoCobiVem).
    - Early circulating tumor DNA (ctDNA)-guided approaches may personalize timing of therapy switches.
    - There is an urgent need for prospective, biomarker-driven sequencing trials tailored to patient history and goals.
  - Resistance to BRAF/MEK inhibitors remains a major challenge; a 2003–2024 bibliometric analysis maps global research trends, key contributors, and emerging themes in resistance.
  - Drug exposure dynamically modulates the immunopeptidome; SILAC-based quantitative immunopeptidomics shows distinct HLA peptide presentation changes in BRAF/MEK inhibitor–sensitive versus –resistant melanoma cells, with implications for immune recognition and therapy combinations.

- Brain metastases management
  - Modalities include ICI, BRAF/MEK inhibitors, stereotactic radiosurgery (SRS), and combinations. Optimal integration, sequencing, and combinations remain unclear and are an active area of investigation.

- Uveal melanoma (French expert consensus)
  - Diagnostics: liver biopsy and imaging, ctDNA analysis, and high-definition HLA typing for HLA-A*02:01.
  - Locoregional liver therapies: surgery for limited disease; isolated hepatic perfusion and chemoembolization for more extensive hepatic involvement.
  - Systemic therapy: tebentafusp is standard of care for HLA-A*02:01–positive patients; for HLA-A*02:01–negative patients with extensive disease, trial enrollment is encouraged; alternatives include percutaneous hepatic perfusion and ICI (alone or combination).
  - Response assessment: RECIST, tumor growth rate (TGR), and ctDNA dynamics.

- Cellular and neoadjuvant therapies
  - Cellular therapy: The first tumor-infiltrating lymphocyte (TIL) product, lifileucil, received FDA approval in 2024 for metastatic melanoma previously treated with anti–PD-1 therapy and (for BRAF V600–mutant disease) a BRAF inhibitor. Ongoing development targets improved safety/efficacy. Additional cellular modalities include TCR-transduced T cells and T-cell engager bispecifics targeting melanoma antigens.
  - Neoadjuvant therapy: Compared with adjuvant therapy, neoadjuvant approaches can induce more robust antitumor immunity, enable better prognostication, and personalize surgical/medical strategies based on on-treatment response; these strategies are reshaping clinical practice.

- Toxicity and supportive care
  - Ocular immune toxicity: Vogt-Koyanagi-Harada (VKH)-like uveitis is uniquely associated with ICI and also reported with BRAF/MEK inhibitors; a systematic review details comorbidities, ocular presentations, management, and visual outcomes.
  - Cutaneous adverse events: A systematic review/meta-analysis compares cutaneous toxicities of combined BRAF/MEK inhibition versus BRAF inhibitor monotherapy.
  - Hepatic complications: Metastatic melanoma to the liver and immunotherapy-induced hepatitis can lead to acute liver failure; case evidence supports considering liver support system devices as temporizing measures in select situations.

- Precision oncol
