Across 20 publications, the literature spans melanoma biology, diagnostics, therapeutics, safety, and regulatory shifts. Key themes: MAPK-pathway oncogenic drivers (BRAF, NRAS, MAP2K1) and parallel networks (PI3K/AKT/mTOR, WNT/β-catenin); evolving systemic strategies (BRAF/MEK inhibitors, immune checkpoint inhibitors, and triplet combinations); variable mutation classes (including non‑V600 BRAF) with unresolved optimal targeting; prognostic/biomarker advances (e.g., SIGLEC8); notable toxicities (inflammatory events, endocrine autoimmunity, mesenteric panniculitis); precision diagnostics (LNA, CDx labeling). Evidence highlights durable benefit from immunotherapy, high response but shorter durability with BRAF/MEK, and a growing emphasis on rational combinations and sequencing.

Paper highlights
- SIGLEC8 and prognosis in skin cutaneous melanoma (SKCM): Elevated SIGLEC8 expression correlates with better prognosis and associates with hematopoietic cell lineage pathways, reinforcing the role of the tumor microenvironment. Current markers (TNM, BRAF status, TMB) lack precision; SIGLEC8’s immune-regulatory impact in SKCM is newly explored.

- Mesenteric panniculitis (MP) with systemic melanoma therapies: Retrospective observational analysis prompted by case signals suggests a correlation between MP incidence and BRAF/MEK inhibitor therapy (BRAFi/MEKi), whereas comparisons with PD‑1/CTLA‑4 ICI are part of the evaluation. Because MP can mimic or mask malignancy, heightened clinical awareness during BRAFi/MEKi is advised.

- BRAF V600E structural/functional review: BRAFV600E creates a constitutively active kinase, driving ligand-independent MAPK signaling and cancer hallmarks. The review describes three BRAF mutation classes (class 1 point mutations, class 2 in-frame indels, class 3 gene fusions/large rearrangements) and pathway dysregulation spanning MAPK/ERK, PI3K/AKT/mTOR, TP53, DNA damage response, and WNT/β‑catenin, with therapeutic implications for pathway-directed combinations.

- MAP2K1-mutated melanocytic tumors: Tumors with MAP2K1 in‑frame deletions show reproducible histopathology and share similarities with BRAF V600E melanocytic neoplasms. Despite frequent spitzoid cytomorphology, placement among Spitz tumors is debated, especially absent additional molecular “hits.”

- Sinonasal mucosal melanoma (n=90): Median age 68; predominantly nasal cavity, epithelioid morphology. Drivers: RAS mutations in 42% (NRAS 36/90), BRAF in 4%. BRAF/RAS mutants were more common in paranasal sinuses (71%) vs nasal (41%). In BRAF/RAS wild-type: NF1 mutations 6% or deletions 4%, SPRED1 12%, PIK3CA 6%, PTEN 8%, mTOR 2%; TP53 deletions 13% and mutations 6%. WNT-pathway alterations: APC 4%, CTNNB1 3%, AMER1 1%. TERT promoter mutations 9% and TERT fusions 5% (the latter in BRAF/RAS wild-type). No cutaneous melanoma fusion transcripts detected. ADCK4::NUMBL cis-fusions in 8 tumors (7 wild-type). Conclusion: NRAS/MAPK-pathway activation predominates; SPRED1 plays a role.

- Non‑V600 BRAF mutations: ~35% of BRAF alterations are non‑V600; many are oncogenic and fall into three molecular classes. Consensus treatment strategies for class 2 and 3 remain undefined; clinical activity of MAPK-targeted regimens varies, underscoring need for mutation class–specific approaches.

- FDA companion diagnostic (CDx) therapeutic class labeling: Post-April 2020 guidance, among 47 FDA-cleared/approved in vitro CDx (as of Jan 2, 2022), only 3 labels employed therapeutic class language: two for EGFR (NSCLC) and one for BRAF V600E and BRAF/MEK inhibitor combinations in melanoma. Two launched with class labeling; one via revision. Of 25 indications, 9 had >1 CDx. Adoption is slow but precedent supports future uptake.

