- Prognosis/biomarkers: Traditional markers (TNM, BRAF status, TMB) are insufficient for precise prediction in cutaneous melanoma. Elevated SIGLEC8 expression associates with better prognosis and links to hematopoietic cell lineage pathway, highlighting tumor microenvironment relevance.
- Molecular drivers and pathways: BRAF V600E is a dominant oncogenic alteration; non-V600 BRAF mutations (≈35% of all BRAF mutations across cancers) remain therapeutically challenging. Melanoma pathobiology involves MAPK/ERK, PI3K/AKT/mTOR, TP53, DNA damage response, and WNT/β-catenin pathways; sinonasal mucosal melanoma is often NRAS-driven with occasional alterations in NF1, SPRED1, PI3K/PTEN/mTOR, WNT regulators, TERT promoter/fusions.
- Therapeutics: ICIs (CTLA-4, PD-1, LAG-3) and BRAF/MEK targeted therapy have transformed outcomes; adjuvant and neoadjuvant use reduces relapse in stage II–III. However, ≥50% of metastatic patients lack durable benefit from ICIs; resistance to BRAF/MEK persists. Emerging options include TIL therapy, T-cell engagers (gp100), and engineered T-cell approaches (TCR, CAR-T, CRISPR). Sequencing data favor starting with ICI before BRAF/MEK in BRAF-mutant metastatic disease. Safety management is critical (uveitis, cardiac effects, other irAEs).
- Technology and care delivery: Teledermatology and AI skin-screening apps may expand access, but real-world validation shows performance drops; focus is shifting to AI that augments clinical decisions and improves outcomes.
- Regulation/diagnostics: FDA CDx labeling now sometimes uses therapeutic class language (e.g., BRAF V600E/BRAF–MEK inhibitor class), though adoption remains limited.

