Epidemiology and mutation spectrum
- BRAF mutations occur in ~40–60% of cutaneous melanomas and activate the MAPK pathway (RAS→BRAF→MEK→ERK), promoting proliferation and survival.
- Croatian cohort (2017–2022; n=179 stage III/IV): BRAF mutations in 48.6% (87/179); NRAS in 15.1%; BRAF/NRAS wild-type 36.3%.
  - Among BRAF-mutated cases: V600E/E2/D 70.1% (61/87); V600K 23.0% (20/87); V600R 2.3% (2/87); exon 11 3.4% (3/87); K601E 1.2% (1/87).
  - Thus, V600 mutations comprised 95.4% of BRAF mutations; non-V600 4.6%.
  - Expected lower response to MAPK inhibition flagged in 6/87: V600R (2), K601E (1), exon 11 (3).
- BRAF mutation functional classes:
  - Class I: high kinase activity (e.g., V600 variants); generally sensitive to BRAF±MEK inhibitors.
  - Class II: intermediate activity (e.g., K601E, L597); often resistant to BRAF inhibitor monotherapy but can respond to MEK or combined BRAF/MEK inhibition.
  - Class III: low kinase activity; low response to targeted therapy.

Therapeutic efficacy by mutation
- Common V600E/K:
  - Combined BRAF/MEK inhibition yields high response rates (overall response rate [ORR] ~63–68%) and improves PFS/OS in randomized trials.
- Less common V600 non-E/K:
  - International analysis (Menzer et al., 2019): ORR ~45% to MAPK pathway inhibitors.
- Non-V600 (Class II/III):
  - Menzer et al., 2019: ORR ~18% to MAPK pathway-directed therapy.
  - K601E/L597:
    - MEK inhibitor trametinib shows activity: retrospective series reported responses (3 partial responses among 5 patients; includes L597Q) with manageable toxicity; paired biopsies showed phospho-ERK reduction correlating with metabolic response.
    - Durable complete response (>36 months) to trametinib reported in a K601E case.
    - Dabrafenib plus trametinib reported active in K601E melanoma (case report).
    - Vemurafenib monotherapy lacked benefit in a K601E patient (case report), reinforcing relative resistance to BRAF inhibitor monotherapy in Class II.
- Rare exon 11 and atypical exon 15 mutations: individual case reports discuss potential options; evidence remains limited and individualized.
- Non-V600E/K real-world experience (Bologna, 2011–2021; n=14 stage III/IV): mutations included V600R, V600_K601delinsE, K601E, p.T599_V600insT, L597V, G466R, S467L, A598T (last two novel in melanoma). Treatments: BRAF/MEK combos (4), BRAF monotherapy (2), immune checkpoint inhibitors (ICI) (6), adjuvant nivolumab (4). Data emphasize scarcity of robust, randomized evidence and the need to report outcomes.
- Immunotherapy: remains an important modality; dermatologic immune-related effects include regressed melanocytic nevi observed during pembrolizumab.

Updated multicenter analysis and data resource (2025)
- Menzer et al., 2025 (Eur J Cancer; doi:10.1016/j.ejca.2025.115703) present an updated international dataset on rare BRAF-mutated melanoma with 49 additional patients and announce a publicly accessible global outcomes database to address uncertainties in efficacy for rare mutations.

Basket trials with BRAF/MEK inhibition
- TAPUR (phase II basket) evaluated cobimetinib plus vemurafenib in patients with advanced cancers harboring targetable genomic alterations, including cohorts in solid tumors and colorectal cancer. Specific outcomes are not provided in the abstracts here, but these studies explore activity of BRAF/MEK targeting across histologies.

Biology
- Post-translational regulation: acetylation-dependent mechanisms modulate BRAF oncogenic function (Cell Reports 2022), suggesting additional therapeutic angles beyond kinase inhibition.

