1) Mutations in BRAF codons 594 and 596 predict good prognosis in melanoma (Wu et al., Oncol Lett, 2017; DOI: 10.3892/ol.2017.6608; PubMed: 28927118)
- Population/methods: Non-Caucasian melanoma cohort; exon 15 of BRAF analyzed by PCR in 1,554 patients; 912 had valid follow-up. Patients stratified by BRAF status: wild-type (n=752), V600E (n=147), D594/G596 (n=13).
- Mutation prevalence: 24.3% of patients (377/1,554) harbored BRAF mutations. Three patients had two mutations; total mutations counted = 380.
  - By functional class (out of 380 mutations): kinase-activating 93.4% (355/380), kinase-impairing 3.4% (13/380), unknown effect 3.2% (12/380).
- Outcomes:
  - BRAF V600E associated with poor prognosis.
  - BRAF D594/G596 (kinase-impairing) associated with longer overall survival versus V600E: median OS 45 vs 25 months; HR 0.45 (95% CI, 0.31–0.97); P=0.043.
- Conclusions/implications: In this large non-Caucasian series, melanoma with BRAF codon 594/596 mutations is biologically distinct from V600E-driven disease and confers a more favorable prognosis. These findings support incorporating BRAF mutation type (activating vs impairing) into personalized management strategies.

2) Recent progress in the identification of BRAF inhibitors as anti-cancer agents (El-Nassan, Eur J Med Chem, 2014; DOI: 10.1016/j.ejmech.2013.11.018; PubMed: 24424304)
- Scope: Review of BRAF as a therapeutic target within the RAS/BRAF/MEK/ERK (MAPK) pathway, focusing on discovery and development of BRAF inhibitors (emphasis on the preceding five years).
- Key points:
  - BRAF is frequently mutated in cancers, notably melanoma; BRAF V600E drives MAPK pathway activation and malignant proliferation.
  - Numerous BRAF inhibitors have been discovered; many show potent antitumor activity, especially in tumors harboring BRAF V600E.
  - Several agents entered clinical trials with encouraging outcomes.
- Implication: Validates BRAF (particularly V600E) as a prime oncogenic driver and drug target; underpins the clinical utility of BRAF-directed therapy in appropriate molecular subsets.

3) BRAF L597 mutations in melanoma are associated with sensitivity to MEK inhibitors (Dahlman et al., 2012; PubMed: 22798288)
- Approach: Whole-genome sequencing of an aggressive melanoma negative for BRAF V600 and common KIT mutations revealed a somatic BRAF L597R (exon 15) mutation.
- Expanded screening: Among 49 melanomas negative for BRAF V600, KIT, NRAS, GNAQ, and GNA11 (“pan-negative”), 2 (4%) harbored L597 mutations; two additional tumors had BRAF D594 and K601 mutations.
- Functional/clinical data:
  - In vitro, signaling driven by BRAF L597R/S/Q mutants was suppressed by MEK inhibition.
  - A patient with metastatic melanoma harboring BRAF L597S showed a significant clinical response to the MEK inhibitor TAK-733.
- Conclusion: BRAF L597 mutations, while uncommon, are clinically meaningful and predict sensitivity to MEK inhibitors, suggesting actionable targets beyond V600E.

Integrated takeaways
- BRAF mutation heterogeneity matters:
  - Activating V600E is common and portends poorer prognosis; it is the primary target of approved BRAF inhibitors.
  - Kinase-impaired mutations (e.g., D594, G596) occur infrequently but are associated with better prognosis versus V600E in non-Caucasian cohorts.
  - Non-V600 activating or intermediate mutations (e.g., L597, K601) can drive MAPK signaling and may be responsive to MEK inhibition even when BRAF V600-targeted inhibitors are less applicable.
- Clinical implications:
  - Comprehensive BRAF exon 15 testing (beyond V600) can identify rare but actionable mutations and refine prognosis.
  - Therapeutic selection may differ by BRAF mutation class: BRAF inhibitors for V600E; MEK inhibitors may benefit select non-V600 alterations (e.g., L597), while kinase-impaired mutations (D594/G596) show distinct biology and better outcomes.
