Overall themes
- Kinase fusions involving BRAF and RAF1 are increasingly recognized across melanocytic neoplasms, including non-Spitz cutaneous melanomas and Spitz-spectrum tumors, with important diagnostic, prognostic, and therapeutic implications.
- Fusion-positive melanomas can present without Spitz morphology, occur in adults and children, and may be aggressive. Fusion detection is clinically actionable as emerging RAF pathway inhibitors and MEK inhibitors show activity in select fusion contexts.
- Tumor fusion burden (TFB) is a potential prognostic biomarker: high TFB correlates with chromosomal instability, immune evasion features, poor response to immune checkpoint blockade (ICB), and worse survival.

Clinicopathologic and pediatric insights
- Melanoma arising in congenital giant melanocytic nevi (CGMN): Case series of 3 pediatric patients highlights rarity but aggressive behavior and diagnostic overlap with benign proliferations; integrates clinicopathologic, molecular, and therapeutic profiles, underscoring epigenomic characterization challenges (Miele et al.).
- RAF1 fusion in non-Spitz melanoma: A 24-year-old man with ulcerated nodular melanoma (4.1 mm) and MAP4::RAF1 fusion, triple wild-type (no BRAF/NRAS/KIT), TERT promoter mutation, TMB ≈7/Mb; pT4bN1a; disease refractory to dual immunotherapy and chemotherapy; death at 18 months—expands clinicopathologic/prognostic spectrum of RAF1-fused, non-Spitz melanomas (Do et al.).
- RAF1 and BRAF fusions with metastases: Series of 7 RAF1 and 23 BRAF fusion-positive melanomas; most in adults, often lacking Spitz-like features; fusions are potentially targetable; emphasizes awareness of varied presentations (Lezcano et al.).
- Spitz spectrum nuances:
  - Atypical Spitz tumor with lymph node metastasis in a 10-year-old girl: PRAME-negative; ALK/ROS negative; no BRAF/TERT mutations; no germline high-risk melanoma variants; no recurrence at 20 years—supports generally favorable outcomes of nodal involvement in atypical Spitz tumors vs childhood melanoma with TERT alterations (Schirwani et al.).
  - Collision tumor: Spitz melanocytoma with novel MYH9::LTK fusion plus separate BRAFV600E dermal nevus; BRAFV600E IHC limited to the nevus aided correct diagnosis and avoided misclassification as melanoma with preexisting nevus (Meijs-Hermanns et al.).

Novel and diverse fusions in melanoma
- RNF11::BRAF fusion: Triple wild-type, aggressive cutaneous melanoma with TERT promoter mutation; extensive metastases and death ~58 months after initial diagnosis—first report of RNF11::BRAF in melanoma; partner known in histiocytoses (Gao et al.).
- TMEM106B::BRAF fusion: Late recurrent Spitz melanoma 36 years after initial diagnosis; co-mutations in CHEK2 and MUTYH; supports Spitz lineage and role of molecular profiling (Koga et al.).
- EPS15::BRAF fusion: Melanocytic neoplasm with congenital-type nevoid features, broadening morphologic spectrum beyond Spitzoid lesions (Agrawal & Guo).
- MAP4::RAF1 fusion: Fatal non-Spitz melanoma in young adult (see above).
- BRAF fusion structural diversity: DNA and RNA sequencing reveal structural variability, underscoring the need for comprehensive NGS to define actionable fusion architecture (Yang et al.).
- Broader landscape (truncated abstract title indicates): First reported BRAF kinase domain duplication (KDD), new fusion partners, and clinical outcomes in melanoma (Odintsov et al.).

