Tool `Paper Search:search_pubmed` with Parameter {'query': 'triplet therapy melanoma atezolizumab vemurafenib cobimetinib', 'max_results': 20} on Paper Search succeeded. Result (summarized from 2208 tokens): - TRICOTEL (Dummer et al., 2023; Lancet Oncology, doi:10.1016/S1470-2045(23)00334-0): Multicentre, open-label, single-arm phase 2 study evaluating atezolizumab combined with either cobimetinib or vemurafenib plus cobimetinib in patients with melanoma with CNS metastases, including symptomatic cases. Rationale: both targeted therapy and immunotherapy show intracranial activity, but there is an unmet need, particularly for symptomatic CNS disease.

- Network meta-analysis of first-line advanced melanoma (Boutros et al., 2023; Eur J Cancer, doi:10.1016/j.ejca.2023.04.010): Systematic review and network meta-analysis comparing the activity and safety of available first-line options, prompted by the approval of relatlimab/nivolumab (anti-LAG3 plus anti-PD-1). Notes ipilimumab/nivolumab as the current overall survival benchmark despite high toxicity. For BRAF-mutant patients, BRAF/MEK inhibitors and the atezolizumab/vemurafenib/cobimetinib triplet are available; the analysis informs optimal selection among these regimens.

- IMspire150 overall survival analysis (Ascierto et al., 2023; Lancet Oncology, doi:10.1016/S1470-2045(22)00687-8): Reports overall survival with first-line atezolizumab + vemurafenib + cobimetinib versus placebo + vemurafenib + cobimetinib in BRAF-mutant melanoma. The primary analysis of IMspire150 previously showed improved investigator-assessed progression-free survival with the triplet.

- Biomarkers in IMspire150 (Robert et al., 2022; Ann Oncol, doi:10.1016/j.annonc.2022.01.076): Explores biomarkers associated with benefit from atezolizumab + vemurafenib + cobimetinib versus the doublet. Builds on IMspire150’s finding of significantly improved progression-free survival with the triplet to identify predictors of response.

- Expert review of the triplet first line (Schmitt, Dumas, Larkin, 2022; Expert Rev Anticancer Ther, doi:10.1080/14737140.2022.2017286): Reviews the rationale for combining BRAF/MEK inhibition with immune checkpoint blockade to potentially raise response rates and improve durability. Summarizes IMspire150, which compared atezolizumab + cobimetinib + vemurafenib to cobimetinib + vemurafenib alone in unresectable metastatic BRAF V600–mutated melanoma.

