Tool `Paper Search:search_pubmed` with Parameter {'query': 'COMBI AD melanoma', 'max_results': 20} on Paper Search succeeded. Result (summarized from 5438 tokens): Cross-study summary on adjuvant and systemic therapy in melanoma (2019–2025)

Clinical need and risk stratification
- Stage III melanoma prognosis is heterogeneous. Pivotal adjuvant trials (KEYNOTE-054, CheckMate-238, COMBI-AD) show marked reductions in recurrence with pembrolizumab, nivolumab, and dabrafenib/trametinib, but clinicians lack dynamic tools to assess recurrence risk over time (Boutros et al., 2025).
- Selecting the optimal adjuvant regimen remains challenging despite clear risk reduction (Wohlfeil, 2025).

Efficacy highlights (adjuvant/neoadjuvant)
- Stage III (resected, high risk): Adjuvant pembrolizumab, nivolumab, and dabrafenib/trametinib (D+T) significantly improve relapse-free survival (RFS) and distant metastasis-free survival (DMFS). Reported benchmarks include:
  - COMBI-AD: 5-year RFS ~65% vs 58% (D+T vs placebo) in earlier analyses; final results reported in 2024 confirm long-term benefit (Long et al., 2024; Dummer et al., 2020).
  - KEYNOTE-054: 3-year RFS 63.7% vs 41% (pembrolizumab vs placebo) (Lao et al., 2022).
- Stage II IIB/C: Adjuvant pembrolizumab and nivolumab improve RFS and DMFS, but absolute benefit is modest. Toxicity risk (including long-term endocrine events) necessitates careful risk–benefit discussion; better selection tools are needed (Lee et al., 2023).
- Neoadjuvant immunotherapy for clinically detectable nodal disease shows promising outcomes and may become a standard in selected stage III patients (Lee et al., 2023).

Biomarkers and personalization
- Circulating tumor DNA (ctDNA):
  - Adjuvant setting: Droplet digital PCR (ddPCR) assays for BRAF ctDNA in stage III melanoma predict survival outcomes on targeted therapy or placebo and can identify high- vs low-risk patients for recurrence, supporting risk-adapted adjuvant care (Syeda et al., 2025).
  - Advanced disease: Baseline ctDNA levels and early kinetics during BRAFi/MEKi therapy correlate with survival, supporting ctDNA as a dynamic efficacy biomarker (Syeda et al., 2021).
- Tumor-infiltrating B cells may influence outcomes with D+T, highlighting a potential predictive role beyond T-cell–centric markers (Brase et al., 2021).

Toxicity and management
- Dabrafenib + trametinib pyrexia:
  - Pyrexia is common and led to 9% permanent discontinuations in COMBI-AD.
  - COMBI-APlus showed an adapted pyrexia management algorithm reduced high-grade pyrexia and pyrexia-related adverse outcomes, improving tolerability in the adjuvant setting (Atkinson et al., 2022; Schadendorf et al., 2021).
- Immune checkpoint inhibitor (ICI)–induced insulin-dependent diabetes mellitus (IDDM) is rare but serious:
  - Multicenter cohort (n=34): Median onset 2.4 months from ICI start; earlier with combination anti–PD-1/PD-L1 + CTLA-4 vs monotherapy (1.4 vs 3.9 months). Over half had melanoma (CANDIED cohort, 2021).
- Overall, substantial and sometimes lifelong toxicities in the adjuvant setting warrant careful patient counseling and selection (Lao et al., 2022; Lee et al., 2023).

Surgical paradigm shifts
- Sentinel node–positive patients: Omission of completion lymph node dissection (MSLT-II, DeCOG-SLT) does not compromise survival, reducing surgical morbidity. Modern adjuvant systemic therapy improves RFS by ~10–20% in stage III, reshaping locoregional management (Wysocki et al., 2021).

Health economics and policy
- NCCN guidelines prefer combination immunotherapy over BRAFi/MEKi for first-line unresectable/metastatic melanoma, regardless of BRAF status (Hanna et al., 2025).
- Adjuvant systemic therapy substantially impacts healthcare budgets as more patients become eligible:
  - Europe-wide and German model-based analyses assessed the cost-effectiveness of adjuvant nivolumab and D+T versus observation and compared adverse event costs; results underscore improved RFS at high cost and the importance of value-based selection (Mulder et al., 2021; Wahler et al., 2022; Wahler et al., 2020).
  - Metastatic BRAF-mutant setting: Comparative cost-effectivenes
Tool `Paper Search:search_pubmed` with Parameter {'query': 'neoadjuvant dabrafenib trametinib melanoma', 'max_results': 20} on Paper Search succeeded. Result (summarized from 6377 tokens): Overview
- Focus: Perioperative systemic therapy for cutaneous melanoma—especially BRAF-targeted dabrafenib plus trametinib (D+T) and immune checkpoint inhibitors—across neoadjuvant, adjuvant, and metastatic settings. Increasing evidence supports neoadjuvant strategies in high-risk stage III disease, with evolving biomarkers and open questions about optimal sequencing, duration, and patient selection.

Guidelines and treatment landscape (reviews/chapters)
- Immunotherapy (nivolumab, pembrolizumab; ipilimumab in combination) and BRAF/MEK inhibitors (dabrafenib plus trametinib) improve relapse-free survival (RFS) in resected high-risk stage III melanoma; stage IIB/IIC adjuvant pembrolizumab and nivolumab improve RFS and distant metastasis-free survival (DMFS), but absolute benefit is modest with endocrine toxicity concerns (need for better risk stratification).
- For Stage IV: immunotherapy (nivolumab, pembrolizumab) and MAPK inhibitors for BRAF-mutant disease; BRAF wild-type options include nivolumab–relatlimab and combinations. Tumor-infiltrating lymphocyte (TIL) therapy is emerging.
- Neoadjuvant therapy is increasingly favored for clinically detectable nodal disease; may reduce systemic therapy duration and improve long-term outcomes. COVID-19 accelerated adoption of neoadjuvant treatment in stage III locally advanced melanoma.
- Key gaps: many adjuvant trials used RFS, not overall survival (OS); uncertain benefit in stage IIIA; no validated prospective biomarkers to personalize perioperative therapy; unclear best strategy at relapse; many patients cured by surgery alone—necessitating individualized decisions and consideration of implications for managing recurrence.

Neoadjuvant targeted therapy (BRAF/MEK)
- NeoCombi (phase II): Neoadjuvant D+T in resectable stage III melanoma showed high pathological response and strong short-term outcomes; 5-year results reported, supporting durable benefit.
- REDUCTOR (prospective, single-arm, phase II): Short-term neoadjuvant D+T enabled radical resection in previously unresectable regionally advanced melanoma, supporting cytoreductive use to achieve operability.
- Perioperative cohort (borderline resectable stage III B-D/IV; combination predominantly D+T): At least 12 weeks pre-op targeted therapy followed by ~40 weeks post-op. 78% achieved R0 resection; 10 patients had pathologic complete response (pCR). Major pathologic response (MPR; ≤10% viable tumor) correlated with significantly longer disease-free and progression-free survival. No neoadjuvant discontinuations for toxicity; no new surgery-specific complications. Fourteen recurrences/progressions occurred post-operatively. Supports safety and the role of pathologic response as a surrogate biomarker.
- Case report: Stage IV BRAF(V600) melanoma had rapid regression within 1 week of D+T; at 3 months, PET/CT showed ΔSUVmax −51.9%, ΔMTV −74.5%, ΔTLG −83.5%, indicating excellent response.

Neoadjuvant immunotherapy and combinations
- NeoTrio (non-comparative, randomized, phase II): Tested sequential/combination BRAF-targeted therapy with anti–PD-1 (pembrolizumab) in resectable stage III BRAF-mutant melanoma, based on early immune changes suggesting synergy; aimed to increase the proportion achieving long-term recurrence-free survival with neoadjuvant immunotherapy.
- Systematic review (8 phase II trials; 450 patients): Safest/most efficacious neoadjuvant regimens were D+T and ipilimumab (1 mg/kg) + nivolumab (3 mg/kg). pCR rates and adverse events were comparable between these regimens; ipilimumab + nivolumab showed longer RFS. Emphasized need for predictors of pCR, correlation with long-term outcomes, and phase III comparisons vs standard care.

Dynamic histopathology and early biomarkers in neoadjuvant therapy
- Longitudinal analysis (60 patients; 4 matched neoadjuvant timepoints): Demonstrated dynamic treatment-response features. Females had higher rates of MPR (P = .002) and more inflammatory and hyalinized fibrosis (P = .04; P = .0


