  - Findings: In the US, introduction of birth certificates coincides with a 69–82% drop in supercentenarian records. In Europe, remarkable ages correlate with poverty, low incomes, shorter life expectancy, higher crime, worse health, fewer 90+ residents, and remote/overseas locales. Only 18% of validated supercentenarians have birth certificates (0% in US); birthdates cluster on days divisible by five, indicating fraud/error. Blue zones (Sardinia, Okinawa, Ikaria) align with poorer, lower-longevity regions.
  - Implication: Relative poverty and short lifespan predict “remarkable” ages, supporting a primary role for fraud/error in extreme age records.

