1) Disulfidptosis-related lncRNA signature in melanoma (Lei et al., 2024; TCGA-SKCM)
- Built a 2-lncRNA disulfidptosis-related (DRL) prognostic model and defined a new molecular subtype classification for melanoma.
- The 2-DRL model showed strong survival prediction and ROC performance.
- High-risk group: significantly lower ESTIMATEScore, ImmuneScore, and StromalScore, indicating pronounced immune suppression/exhaustion.
- Subtypes:
  - C2: immune-activated state.
  - C1 and C3: immune suppression/exhaustion, potentially worse prognosis.
- Predicted drug sensitivities by subtype:
  - C1: Zoledronate, UMI-77, Nilotinib, Cytarabine.
  - C2: Ribociclib, XAV939, Topotecan, Ruxolitinib.
  - C3: VX-11e, Ulixertinib, Trametinib, Afatinib.
- Implications: clarifies tumor immune microenvironment variation and suggests subtype-specific targeted therapies; identifies DRL targets for further study.

2) ERK inhibition in metastatic uveal melanoma (Buchbinder et al., 2024; Phase II, ulixertinib/BVD-523)
- Rationale: >80% of uveal melanomas harbor GNAQ/GNA11 mutations driving MAPK signaling.
- Ulixertinib is a potent, reversible, ATP-competitive ERK1/2 inhibitor.
- Study investigates efficacy/safety of ERK inhibition in this genomically defined melanoma subset.

3) Case report: ERK1/2 inhibitor ulixertinib in BRAF D594G cutaneous melanoma (Wolfe et al., 2022)
- Patient with stage III, non-V600 BRAF D594G melanoma had an extraordinary response to ulixertinib as fourth-line therapy.
- Accessed via an intermediate expanded access protocol allowing single-agent/combination use, dose adjustments, treatment breaks for surgery, and long-term preventive therapy post-resection.
- Outcome suggests potential for curative intent in select non-V600 BRAF-mutant cases with ERK inhibition.

4) Dual HDAC and mTOR inhibition in uveal melanoma models (Patel et al., 2021)
- Uveal melanomas frequently carry GNAQ/GNA11 variants activating survival pathways.
- Building on prior data in RAS-mutant models, combining survival pathway inhibitors with the HDAC inhibitor romidepsin was tested.
- Dual inhibition of HDAC and mTOR promoted apoptosis in uveal melanoma cell lines, supporting combination strategies.

5) CDK4/6 as a therapeutic vulnerability in BRAF/MEK inhibitor-resistant melanoma (Nassar et al., 2021)
- Addressing resistance to combined BRAF/MEK inhibition, the study used whole-exome sequencing to identify drivers/biomarkers.
- Findings support targeting CDK4/6 as a salvage strategy in acquired resistance settings.

6) ERK1/2 biology and inhibitor landscape (Roskoski, 2019; review)
- ERK1/2 are central nodes in the Ras–Raf–MEK–ERK cascade, regulating proliferation, apoptosis, immunity, and transcription.
- Activation: MEK1/2 sequentially phosphorylate ERK1/2 (Y204/187 then T202/185). ERK1/2 are proline-directed kinases (PxS/TP motif).
- Inactivation via dual-specificity, tyrosine-specific, and serine/threonine phosphatases; signaling is highly dynamic (tens of thousands of phosphorylation events in cell cycle).
- Clinically, only BRAF/MEK combinations are approved for BRAF-mutant melanoma (dabrafenib+trametinib, encorafenib+binimetinib, vemurafenib+cobimetinib), but resistance typically arises within ~1 year.
- ERK inhibitors (ulixertinib, MK-8353, GDC-0994) are in early trials and can overcome BRAF/MEK inhibitor resistance in models.
- MK-8353 illustrates an ERK dual-mechanism paradigm: reduces RSK phosphorylation (via ERK inhibition) and ERK phosphorylation (MEK cannot phosphorylate ERK when ERK is bound by MK-8353).

7) Preclinical discovery of ulixertinib (BVD-523) (Germann et al., 2017)
