Tool `Paper Search:search_pubmed` with Parameter {'query': 'IMspire150 overall survival 2023 atezolizumab vemurafenib cobimetinib melanoma', 'max_results': 10} on Paper Search succeeded. Result (summarized from 1105 tokens): Summary of included studies on BRAF-mutant advanced/metastatic melanoma

1) Dummer R, Welti M, Ramelyte E (2023). The role of triple therapy and therapy sequence in treatment of BRAF-mutant metastatic melanoma. Commentary responding to IMspire150 second interim OS analysis.
- Context: ICI works regardless of mutational status; BRAF/MEK inhibitors are effective for BRAF-mutant disease.
- Triple therapy (anti–PD-1/PD-L1 + BRAF + MEK) vs targeted therapy (TT) alone: across three trials, duration of response improved significantly, but progression-free survival (PFS) and overall survival (OS) were only numerically better, if at all.
- One triple combination has been approved for BRAF-mutant metastatic melanoma; however, the anticipated biological synergy has not been consistently confirmed; benefits may hinge on statistical considerations.
- Treatment sequencing: Two prospective trials compared first-line ICI followed by TT at progression versus the reverse; the SECOMBIT trial also tested a “sandwich” approach (8 weeks TT → ICI until progression → TT). Both studies favored first-line ICI; SECOMBIT showed the sandwich approach had similar benefit.
- Current recommendation: immunotherapy-based first-line regimens (or possibly the sandwich approach). Whether triple therapy is superior to ICI alone remains unresolved and must weigh efficacy and safety.
- DOI: 10.1186/s12967-023-04391-1. PubMed: https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/37543586/

2) Li SN, Wan X, Peng LB, et al. (2023). Cost-effectiveness of immune checkpoint inhibition and targeted treatment in combination for BRAF-mutant advanced melanoma.
- Rationale: Novel agents improve outcomes but are costly; value assessment is needed.
- Objective: Compare cost-effectiveness of first-line options for BRAF-mutant metastatic melanoma, including atezolizumab+vemurafenib+cobimetinib, vemurafenib+cobimetinib, dabrafenib+trametinib, and encorafenib+binimetinib.
- Methods/results not provided in the excerpt; study focuses on effectiveness and cost trade-offs.
- DOI: 10.1186/s12913-023-09058-7. PubMed: https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/36653848/

3) Ascierto PA, Stroyakovskiy D, Gogas H, et al. (2023). Overall survival with first-line atezolizumab + vemurafenib + cobimetinib in BRAF V600-mutant advanced melanoma (IMspire150): second interim analysis.
- Design: Phase 3, multicenter, randomized, first-line; atezolizumab+vemurafenib+cobimetinib vs placebo+vemurafenib+cobimetinib in BRAF V600 mutation–positive advanced melanoma.
- Prior finding (primary analysis): improved investigator-assessed PFS with the triple combination.
- Current focus: second interim OS analysis (details not provided in the excerpt).
- DOI: 10.1016/S1470-2045(22)00687-8. PubMed: https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/36460017/

Integrated takeaways
- Triple therapy can prolong response duration over TT alone, but consistent, clinically meaningful PFS/OS advantages are not established across trials; safety and biological synergy remain in question.
- One triple regimen (atezolizumab+vemurafenib+cobimetinib) has regulatory approval; IMspire150 provides PFS benefit, with ongoing OS evaluation.
- Sequencing matters: First-line ICI generally outperforms first-line TT in prospective trials; a short “sandwich” TT lead-in may be comparable to ICI-first.
- Cost-effectiveness is a key consideration among first-line options (triple therapy and various BRAF/MEK combinations), but specific economic outcomes were not available in the provided text.
Tool `Paper Search:search_pubmed` with Parameter {'query': 'Modern Pathology melanoma fusion trametinib AGAP3::BRAF 2022', 'max_results': 10} on Paper Search succeeded. Result: [{"paper_id":"35871080","title":"Identification of fusions with potential clinical significance in melanoma.","authors":"Moran JMT; Le LP; Nardi V; Golas J; Farahani AA; Signorelli S; Onozato ML; Foreman RK; Duncan LM; Lawrence DP; Lennerz JK; Dias-Santagata D; Hoang MP","abstract":"Though uncommon in melanoma, gene fusions may have therapeutic implications. Next generation sequencing-based clinical assays, designed to detect relevant gene fusions, mutations, and copy number changes, were performed on 750 melanomas (375 primary and 375 metastases) at our institution from 2014-2021. These included 599 (80%) cutaneous, 38 (5%) acral, 11 (1.5%) anorectal, 23 (3%) sinonasal, 27 (3.6%) eye (uveal/ conjunctiva), 11 (1.5%) genital (vulva/penile), and 41 (5.5%) melanomas of unknown primary. Sixteen fusions (2%) were detected in samples from 16 patients: 12/599 (2%) cutaneous, 2/38 (5%) acral, 1/9 (11%) vulva, 1/23(4.3%) sinonasal; and 12/16 (75%) fusions were potentially targetable. We identified two novel rearrangements: NAGS::MAST2 and NOTCH1::GNB1; and two fusions that have been reported in other malignancies but not in melanoma: CANT1::ETV4 (prostate cancer) and CCDC6::RET (thyroid cancer). Additional fusions, previously reported in melanoma, included: EML4::ALK, MLPH::ALK, AGAP3::BRAF, AGK::BRAF, CDH3::BRAF, CCT8::BRAF, DIP2B::BRAF, EFNB1::RAF1, LRCH3::RAF1, MAP4::RAF1, RUFY1::RAF1, and ADCY2::TERT. Fusion positive melanomas harbored recurrent alterations in TERT and CDKN2A, among others. Gene fusions were exceedingly rare (0.2%) in BRAF/RAS/NF1-mutant tumors and were detected in 5.6% of triple wild-type melanomas. Interestingly, gene rearrangements were significantly enriched within the subset of triple wild-type melanomas that harbor TERT promoter mutations (18% versus 2%, p < 0.0001). Thirteen (81%) patients were treated with immunotherapy for metastatic disease or in the adjuvant setting. Six of 12 (50%) patients with potentially actionable fusions progressed on immunotherapy, and 3/6 (50%) were treated with targeted agents (ALK and MEK inhibitors), 2 off-label and 1 as part of a clinical trial. One patient with an AGAP3::BRAF fusion positive melanoma experienced a 30-month long response to trametinib. We show that, detecting fusions, especially in triple wild-type melanomas with TERT promoter mutations, may have a clinically significant impact in patients with advanced disease who have failed front-line immunotherapy.","doi":"10.1038/s41379-022-01138-z","published_date":"2022-01-01T00:00:00","pdf_url":"","url":"https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/35871080/","source":"pubmed","updated_date":"2022-01-01T00:00:00","categories":"","keywords":"","citations":0,"references":"","extra":""}]
Tool `Paper Search:search_pubmed` with Parameter {'query': 'Buchbinder ulixertinib uveal melanoma phase II 2024', 'max_results': 10} on Paper Search succeeded. Result: [{"paper_id":"38683104","title":"A Phase II Study of ERK Inhibition by Ulixertinib (BVD-523) in Metastatic Uveal Melanoma.","authors":"Buchbinder EI; Cohen JV; Tarantino G; Lian CG; Liu D; Haq R; Hodi FS; Lawrence DP; Giobbie-Hurder A; Knoerzer D; Sullivan RJ","abstract":"Uveal melanoma is a rare and aggressive subset of melanoma that is minimally responsive to traditional therapies. Greater than 80% of uveal melanomas have a mutation in GNAQ or GNA11 which lead to downstream signaling through the MAPK pathway. Ulixertinib (BVD-523) is a potent and reversible small-molecule ATP-competitive inhibitor of both ERK1 and ERK2 protein kinases.","doi":"10.1158/2767-9764.CRC-24-0036","published_date":"2024-01-01T00:00:00","pdf_url":"","url":"https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/38683104/","source":"pubmed","updated_date":"2024-01-01T00:00:00","categories":"","keywords":"","citations":0,"references":"","extra":""}]
