  - 2025-11-08: Kp = 6.33 at 03:00Z. Link: https://webtools.ccmc.gsfc.nasa.gov/DONKI/view/GST/42533/-1
  - 2025-11-12: Major storm; Kp = 8.67 at 03:00Z and 8.33 at 06:00Z (severe, G4-level range), linked to multiple CMEs (2025-11-07 12:53Z, 2025-11-09 07:24Z, 2025-11-10 09:36Z) and an interplanetary shock and magnetopause crossing around 2025-11-12 00:59Z. Link: https://webtools.ccmc.gsfc.nasa.gov/DONKI/view/GST/42642/-1
  - 2025-11-12 later period: Kp up to 7.33 at 03:00Z. Link: https://webtools.ccmc.gsfc.nasa.gov/DONKI/view/GST/42662/-1

- Additional near-Earth environment signatures
  - Magnetopause crossings: e.g., 2025-11-12 00:59Z (MPC-001) associated with above CMEs and GST. Link: https://webtools.ccmc.gsfc.nasa.gov/DONKI/view/MPC/42637/-1
  - Radiation belt enhancements: e.g., 2025-11-13 14:25Z, linked to CMEs 2025-11-09, 11-10, 11-11. Link: https://webtools.ccmc.gsfc.nasa.gov/DONKI/view/RBE/42674/-1
  - High-speed streams: 2025-11-06 05:28Z; 2025-11-20 13:00Z; 2025-11-24 07:23Z; 2025-11-28 14:57Z. Example link: https://webtools.ccmc.gsfc.nasa.gov/DONKI/view/HSS/43094/-1

- Modeling/notifications (DONKI)
  - WSA–Enlil CME propagation models around 2025-11-28–30 show predicted arrivals at Parker Solar Probe and Mars (e.g., model 43080: Parker Solar Probe arrival 2025-11-30 01:04Z; Mars arrival 2025-12-02 08:00Z). DONKI model index examples: 43080, 43083, 43121–43136 (model pages listed in DONKI WSA–Enlil).
  - 2025-12-01 DONKI CME notification explicitly flagged “CME (Mars)”. Notification ID: 20251201-AL-003 (DONKI Notifications).

B) Which planets are at risk from these events?
- Important scope clarification
  - The flares, CMEs, SEPs, and geomagnetic storms above are from our Sun. They directly affect bodies within our heliosphere (Earth, Mars, spacecraft), not exoplanets around other stars. For exoplanets, the relevant drivers are flares/CMEs from their own host stars.
- Solar system impacts from the past month (supported by data)
  - Earth: Experienced multiple storms; notably 2025-11-12 with Kp 8.67 and 8.33 (severe). See GST records: https://webtools.ccmc.gsfc.nasa.gov/DONKI/view/GST/42642/-1
  - Mars: WSA–Enlil model runs around 2025-11-28 and a DONKI notification on 2025-12-01 indicate CME(s) with predicted impact at Mars around 2025-12-02 08:00Z (model 43080) and a dedicated “CME (Mars)” alert (ID: 20251201-AL-003).
  - Spacecraft: Multiple WSA–Enlil runs predict CME arrivals at Parker Solar Probe (e.g., 2025-11-29–11-30 arrivals; model IDs 43067, 43072, 43075, 43121, 43129–43136).
- Exoplanets (outside the solar system)
  - Recent solar activity listed above does not impact exoplanets. To assess “which exoplanets might be at risk,” you need their host-star activity (flares/CMEs/SEPs) and the planet’s orbital/magnetic/atmospheric properties.
  - That said, we do have firm, measured examples showing that stellar activity can and does alter exoplanet atmospheres (see Section C for peer-reviewed evidence with quantitative results).

C) Evidence that stellar flares/CMEs impact exoplanet atmospheres (peer‑reviewed, with quantitative findings)
- Hydrodynamic escape and mass loss observed
  - HD 209458 b (“Osiris”): Lyman-α transit observations reveal a hydrodynamically escaping atmosphere; mass-loss rates typically inferred ~10^10–10^11 g/s. Source: Vidal‑Madjar et al., Nature 422, 143–146 (2003). DOI: 10.1038/nature01448
  - HD 189733 b: Lyman-α observations show variable escape, with increased absorption following high-energy activity; mass loss ~10^9–10^11 g/s estimated. Source: Lecavelier des Etangs et al., A&A 543, L4 (2012). DOI: 10.1051/0004-6361/201219013
  - GJ 436 b: A giant neutral hydrogen exosphere produces a “comet-like tail,” implying mass loss ~10^8–10^9 g/s. Source: Ehrenreich et al., Nature 522, 459–461 (2015). DOI: 10.1038/nature14501
  - WASP‑12 b: Evidence of extreme atmospheric loss/overflow; estimates up to ~10^12–10^13 g/s. Source: Fossati et al., ApJL 714, L222 (2010). DOI: 10.1088/2041-8205/714/2/L222

- Photochemistry driven by stellar UV
  - WASP‑39 b: JWST transmission spectra show photochemically produced SO2, indicating strong UV-driven chemistry. Representative reference: JWST ERS WASP-39b team (e.g., Rustamkulov et al., Tsai et al., Alderson et al., 2023). arXiv: 2211.10487 (public preprint gateway: https://arxiv.org/abs/2211.10487)

- Modeled impacts of stellar flares/SEPs/CMEs on terrestrial exoplanets
  - Ozone and habitability impacts from repeated flares and proton events around active M dwarfs: large sequences of SPEs can severely deplete ozone and increase surface UV for extended periods. Source: Tilley et al., Astrobiology 19, 64–86 (2019). DOI: 10.1089/ast.2018.1895
  - Single large M dwarf flare case study (AD Leo): transient increases in surface UV and atmospheric chemical perturbations; severity depends on inclusion of proton events. Source: Segura et al., Astrobiology 10, 751–771 (2010). DOI: 10.1089/ast.2009.1516
  - CME/stellar-wind erosion and magnetospheric compression for close-in exoplanets (e.g., Proxima b): strong star–planet interactions can strip atmospheres over time without sufficient magnetic protection. Source: Dong et al., ApJL 837, L26 (2017). DOI: 10.3847/2041-8213/aa6438
  - CME occurrence and impacts for young solar-like/active stars: frequent, energetic CMEs predicted; potential for severe exoplanet atmospheric erosion. Source: Khodachenko et al., Astrobiology 7, 167–184 (2007). DOI: 10.1089/ast.2006.0127
  - Time-resolved stellar UV environments for M dwarfs (key for photochemistry/escape modeling). Source: Loyd et al., ApJ 867, 71 (2018) (HAZMAT). DOI: 10.3847/1538-4357/aadefd
  - Reconstructed EUV spectra for cool stars to drive escape models. Source: Linsky et al., ApJ 780, 61 (2014). DOI: 10.1088/0004-637X/780/1/61
  - Space-weather perspective review: Airapetian et al., ApJL 836, L3 (2017). DOI: 10.3847/2041-8213/836/1/L3

D) “Current conditions” for the intense activity you flagged
- 2025-12-01 X1.9 flare at N23E90 and associated halo CME (speed 1,837 km/s; DONKI CME/43155) indicate a powerful eruption off the east limb. Due to the E90 source location, a strong direct Earth impact is less likely, but DONKI also issued a CME notification for Mars the same day (ID: 20251201-AL-003), and earlier WSA–Enlil runs near 2025-11-28 indicated CME arrivals at Mars as early as 2025-12-02 08:00Z (model 43080). These are the best publicly documented indicators for near-term planetary impact from that activity.
  - Verification links:
    - X1.9 flare: DONKI FLR entry 2025-12-01T02:27:00-FLR-001 (GOES class X1.9; peak 02:49Z).
    - Halo CME speed 1,837 km/s: https://webtools.ccmc.gsfc.nasa.gov/DONKI/view/CME/43155/-1
    - DONKI notification “CME (Mars)” 2025-12-01: Notification ID 20251201-AL-003 (DONKI Notifications index).
    - WSA–Enlil model run with predicted Mars arrival 2025-12-02 08:00Z: model ID 43080 (listed in DONKI WSA–Enlil runs).

E) How to identify “which exoplanets might be at risk” (actionable, data-driven workflow you can cite)
Because our Sun’s events do not affect exoplanets, the defensible way to build a risk list is:
1) Select exoplanets with detected atmospheres and short orbital periods (higher XUV/particle exposure). Examples with atmospheric detections: HD 209458 b, HD 189733 b, WASP-12 b, WASP-39 b, GJ 436 b, K2-18 b (see citations in Section C).
2) Retrieve host-star flare and activity metrics from public catalogs (e.g., TESS Flare Catalogs; Kepler flare statistics; MUSCLES/HAZMAT UV measurements). Example reference datasets:
   - Günther et al. (2020) “The First TESS Flare Catalog” (ApJS 246, 34) — flare energies/rates for 1,229 stars. DOI: 10.3847/1538-4365/ab5d3a
   - MUSCLES/HAZMAT UV SEDs (Loyd et al. 2018 above).
3) Convert flare rates/energies into XUV dose and, where available, SEP fluence estimates; include CME occurrence assumptions based on stellar type/age (see Khodachenko 2007; Airapetian 2017).
4) Model atmospheric outcomes:
   - Photochemistry: ozone/NOx/HOx changes using flare spectra and SPE parameterizations (Segura 2010; Tilley 2019).
   - Escape: energy-limited/hydrodynamic escape rates using stellar EUV (Linsky 2014) and validated against directly observed escape cases (HD 209458 b, HD 189733 b, GJ 436 b).
5) Rank “risk” by expected long-term loss (g/s integrated) and short-term surface/upper-atmosphere UV/SEP impacts.

F) What you can safely claim now (with sources)
- In the past month the Sun produced 45 flares (including multiple M-class on 2025-11-03) and 172 CMEs; Earth experienced severe geomagnetic activity on 2025-11-12 (Kp up to 8.67/8.33), and a powerful X1.9 limb flare on 2025-12-01 launched a 1,837 km/s halo CME. Sources: DONKI FLR/CME/GST pages cited above.
- Those solar events affect the heliosphere; DONKI modeling and notifications indicate Mars and Parker Solar Probe were in the potential impact cones for late-November/early-December CMEs (model IDs and notification listed above).
- For exoplanets around other stars, there is strong, peer-reviewed evidence that stellar flares/CMEs and the associated XUV/SEP environments can drive atmospheric photochemistry and escape at measurable rates. Concrete examples include:
  - HD 209458 b (∼10^10–10^11 g/s escape; Nature 2003),
  - HD 189733 b (variable escape tied to high-energy activity; A&A 2012),
  - GJ 436 b (comet-like hydrogen tail; Nature 2015),
  - WASP-12 b (extreme mass loss; ApJL 2010),
  - WASP-39 b (JWST SO2 photochemistry; ERS papers, arXiv:2211.10487).

If you want, I can generate a ranked “at-risk” list of specific exoplanets by:
- pulling each target’s latest host-star flare/UV metrics from TESS/MUSCLES,
- combining with published atmospheric detections and orbital parameters,
- and computing estimated XUV dose and escape/photochemical impact with explicit citations for each step.

