Peer-reviewed literature for exoplanet atmospheric effects
- Vidal‑Madjar et al. 2003 (HD 209458 b escape) Nature 422, 143–146. DOI: 10.1038/nature01448
- Lecavelier des Etangs et al. 2012 (HD 189733 b escape) A&A 543, L4. DOI: 10.1051/0004-6361/201219013
- Ehrenreich et al. 2015 (GJ 436 b tail) Nature 522, 459–461. DOI: 10.1038/nature14501
- Fossati et al. 2010 (WASP‑12 b loss) ApJL 714, L222. DOI: 10.1088/2041-8205/714/2/L222
- JWST WASP‑39 b SO2 (ERS team, 2023). arXiv: 2211.10487 (https://arxiv.org/abs/2211.10487)
- Tilley et al. 2019 (repeated flares/SEPs, ozone loss) Astrobiology 19, 64–86. DOI: 10.1089/ast.2018.1895
- Segura et al. 2010 (M‑dwarf flare impacts) Astrobiology 10, 751–771. DOI: 10.1089/ast.2009.1516
- Dong et al. 2017 (Proxima b space weather) ApJL 837, L26. DOI: 10.3847/2041-8213/aa6438
- Khodachenko et al. 2007 (stellar CMEs & exoplanets) Astrobiology 7, 167–184. DOI: 10.1089/ast.2006.0127
- Loyd et al. 2018 (M‑dwarf UV; HAZMAT) ApJ 867, 71. DOI: 10.3847/1538-4357/aadefd
- Linsky et al. 2014 (EUV reconstructions) ApJ 780, 61. DOI: 10.1088/0004-637X/780/1/61

Notes on data gaps
- Several DONKI endpoints experienced temporary connection errors during our retrieval attempts (especially for early December SEP/GST/MPC). Where that occurred, I relied on DONKI events successfully retrieved for mid/late November and on individual event pages that are available and citable. The key high-impact events (Nov 12 geomagnetic storm; Dec 1 X1.9 flare and halo CME; Nov 30 fast CME; Nov 10–11 SEPs) are all backed by direct DONKI links above.

If you share a list of exoplanet targets (or host stars) for your report, I can run the flare/UV cross-matching and produce a ranked, fully cited “risk from stellar activity” table with specific numbers (flare energies/rates, estimated XUV dose, escape rates and photochemical outcomes).
