Tool `National Parks:getParkDetails` with Parameter {'parkCode': 'redw'} on National Parks succeeded. Result (summarized from 3639 tokens): - Name: Redwood National and State Parks (code: redw)
- Designation: National and State Parks
- Website: https://www.nps.gov/redw/index.htm
- Description: Renowned for the world’s tallest trees, with landscapes ranging from open prairies and oak woodlands to pristine rivers and rugged coastline. This ancient homeland has supported people for thousands of years. The National Park Service and California State Parks co-manage the area to safeguard and rejuvenate these lands for public enjoyment, learning, and inspiration.
- State: CA

Location and Directions
- Coordinates: 41.37237268, -124.0318129
- Address: 1111 Second Street, Crescent City, CA 95531
- Region: Northernmost coastal California, near the Oregon border; park corridor is about 60 miles long with four visitor centers north to south.
- Drive times: ~6–7 hours (325 mi) north of San Francisco; ~6 hours (330 mi) south of Portland, OR; ~4 hours (170 mi) west of Redding, CA.
- Directions info: http://www.nps.gov/redw/planyourvisit/directions.htm

Contacts
- Phone (Voice): 7074646101
- Fax: 7074641812
- Email: redw_info@nps.gov

Operating Hours
- Parks are always open; roads, trails, and public access remain open year-round.
- Some campgrounds and visitor centers are closed or have reduced hours in the off-season (October–May).
- Standard hours: All Day, every day (Mon–Sun).

Fees and Passes
- Entrance fees: None listed.
- Entrance passes: None listed.

Weather
- Generally cool and damp; dress in layers and expect wet conditions.
- Typical year-round temps along the redwood coast: mid-40s°F (7°C) to mid-60s°F (18°C).
- Summer: often foggy; highs can occasionally reach low 70s°F (~20°C).
- Winter: cooler with considerable rain.
- Rainfall (Oct–Apr): averages 60–80 inches across the region.

Topics (themes)
- Colonization and Settlement; Farming and Agriculture; Ranches; Industry; Mills; Logging and Timber Cutting; Maritime; Coastal Defenses; Native American Heritage; Social Movements; Conservation Movement; Transportation; Roads, Routes and Highways; Tragic Events; Massacres; Wars and Conflicts; Tribal Conflicts; Indian and Frontier Wars; Westward Expansion; Homesteading; Animals; Birds; Cats (wild); Elk; Fish; Climate Change; Fire; Forests and Woodlands; Coniferous Forests; Geology; Groundwater; Grasslands; Prairies; Oceans; Whales; River and Riparian; Scenic Views; Unique Species; Rare; Endangered; Watersheds; Headwaters; Wilderness.

Activities
- Arts and Culture; Cultural Demonstrations; Auto and ATV; Scenic Driving; Biking; Mountain Biking; Road Biking; Camping; Backcountry Camping; Car or Front Country Camping; Horse Camping (see also Horse/Stock Use); RV Camping; Fishing; Saltwater Fishing; Food; Picnicking; Guided Tours; Self-Guided Tours - Walking; Self-Guided Tours - Auto; Hiking; Backcountry Hiking; Front-Country Hiking; Horse Trekking; Horse Camping (see also camping); Horseback Riding; Paddling; Canoeing; Kayaking; Stand Up Paddleboarding; Whitewater Rafting; Junior Ranger Program; Surfing; Swimming; Saltwater Swimming; Wildlife Watching; Birdwatching; Park Film; Shopping; Bookstore and Park Store.

Images
- 26 official images showcasing redwood groves and canopy views, scenic drives (e.g., Howland Hill Road), coastline and tidepools (Enderts Beach), waterfalls (Trillium Falls), Fern Canyon, ranger programs, walkways (Lady Bird Johnson Grove), wildlife and habitats (nurse logs, elk), seasonal scenes, and family-friendly activities.
