2025-12-01 11:28:23,840 - benchmark.runner - INFO - Standout artifact to anchor your project
- Marble grave stele of a little girl (The Metropolitan Museum of Art, Greek and Roman Art, object ID 252890)
  - Medium: Marble, Parian
  - Dimensions: H. 31 3/4 in. (80.6 cm); width at top 14 9/16 in. (37 cm); width at base 15 1/2 × 4 in. (39.4 × 10.2 cm); 131 lb (59.4 kg)
  - Credit line: Fletcher Fund, 1927
  - Tags: Doves, Girls
  - Image: https://images.metmuseum.org/CRDImages/gr/original/DT279.jpg

Why this piece matters in ancient Greek culture
- Function and setting: Grave stelai marked burials and commemorated the deceased in Classical-period cemeteries around Athens. They were focal points for family remembrance and ritual.
- Poignant subject: The relief shows a young girl tenderly holding her pet doves—an intimate, domestic image that underscores the tragedy of a child’s death and the value Greeks placed on oikos (household) bonds.
- Iconography:
  - Doves can signal purity, affection, and the fragility of life; in a funerary context they sharpen the memorial’s quiet pathos.
  - The child’s gesture and downward gaze exemplify Classical restraint and the Greek ideal of “quiet dignity” in death.
- Style and material: Carved in fine Parian marble, the refined drapery and naturalistic anatomy are hallmarks of Classical sculpture. The relief would originally have had traces of color, making the scene more vivid at the graveside.
- Social meaning: Female (and especially child) commemoration frequently stresses family identity and the domestic sphere rather than public roles, revealing how Athenians framed virtue, affection, and memory by gender and age.

How to connect it to related Met objects for a stronger narrative
Use these as immediate comparanda to show the range of Attic funerary imagery and ritual surrounding death, especially for women and children:
- Marble stele (grave marker) of a young girl (ID 248499)
  - Medium: Marble, Pentelic; Tags: Pomegranates, Girls
  - Image: https://images.metmuseum.org/CRDImages/gr/original/GR43.jpg
  - Why it helps: The pomegranate is a potent funerary symbol (Persephone/underworld); compare its symbolism to the doves on the “little girl” stele.

- Marble stele (grave marker) of a youth and a little girl (ID 248500)
  - Medium: Marble; Tags: Girls, Men, Sphinx
  - Image: https://images.metmuseum.org/CRDImages/gr/original/DP-23253-001.jpg
  - Why it helps: Shows intergenerational commemoration; the sphinx motif evokes older grave-marker traditions and beliefs surrounding the tomb.

- Marble funerary statues of a maiden and a little girl (ID 254508)
  - Medium: Marble, Pentelic
  - Image: https://images.metmuseum.org/CRDImages/gr/original/DP-19803-001.jpg
  - Why it helps: A sculptural group instead of a relief; reinforces themes of female virtue and familial memory.

- Marble funerary lekythos (ID 248644)
  - Medium: Marble (a monumental “lekythos”-shaped grave marker); Tags include Girls, Fathers, Mothers, Women
  - Image: https://images.metmuseum.org/CRDImages/gr/original/DP-23255-001.jpg
  - Why it helps: Bridges the relief tradition with the vessel form most associated with burial rites, letting you talk about ritual practice at the grave.

- Terracotta lekythoi (oil flasks) used in funerary contexts:
  - Eretria Painter (ID 253351) — Image: https://images.metmuseum.org/CRDImages/gr/original/DP292643.jpg
  - Achilles Painter (ID 250550) — Image: https://images.metmuseum.org/CRDImages/gr/original/DP160860.jpg
  - Lecce Painter (ID 248809; includes Hermes) — Image: https://images.metmuseum.org/CRDImages/gr/original/DT223433.jpg
  - Why they help: Lekythoi were closely tied to funerary ritual (oil offerings, grave deposits). The Lecce Painter example includes Hermes—often psychopompos (guide of souls)—giving you a mythic link to afterlife beliefs.

- Small figures that echo childhood/domestic ideals:
  - Terracotta statuette of a standing girl (ID 250741) — https://images.metmuseum.org/CRDImages/gr/original/DP341862.jpg
  - Marble statuette of a girl (ID 252553) — https://images.metmuseum.org/CRDImages/gr/original/149688.jpg
  - Why they help: Reinforce how Greek art idealizes girlhood and domestic virtues beyond funerary monuments.

Key talking points you can cite
- The Met’s object data confirms the “little girl” stele’s material (Parian marble), scale, and iconography (doves), grounding your analysis in primary museum metadata.
- A comparative arc: private/domestic virtue (child + pets) → family remembrance (group reliefs) → ritual vessels at the tomb (lekythoi) → mythic framing of death (Hermes as guide).
- Continuity and change: From earlier markers (sphinx associations) to high Classical naturalism and refined pathos; original polychromy (lost) would have made these memorials visually striking in their cemetery context.

Wikipedia topics to link for context
- “Grave Stele of a Little Girl” (Met piece; article focuses on the doves and Classical relief style)
- “Stele of Hegeso” (benchmark Attic grave relief; ideal comparison for composition, drapery, and domestic themes)
- “Funerary art in ancient Greece” (overview of practices, monuments, and iconography)
- “Lekythos” and “White-ground technique” (vessels and painting style central to funerary ritual)
- “Parian marble” (material prized for fine carving)
- “Naiskos” (the shrine-like architectural frame often used in grave reliefs)
- “Hermes Psychopompos” or “Psychopomp” (mythic guide of souls; ties to lekythoi imagery)
- “Sphinx (Greek mythology)” (role of the sphinx in funerary contexts and tomb markers)

How to cite or find the museum records quickly
- Use each Met object’s ID in the Met collection search (e.g., search “Met 252890”) to access the full object page and label text.
- Images provided above are the Met’s primary image URLs for those records.

If you want a one-piece thesis
- Center your paper on the Marble grave stele of a little girl (ID 252890) as a compact expression of Classical Athenian values surrounding childhood, family, and death.
- Support it with:
  - Symbolic contrast: doves (252890) vs. pomegranate (248499)
  - Family commemoration across ages/sexes (248500, 254508)
  - Ritual practice at the graveside via lekythoi (248644, 253351, 250550, 248809)
- Conclude with broader cultural meaning using the Wikipedia topics above to situate the Met evidence within the wider scholarship on Greek funerary art.
