 FINAL SOLUTION:
2025-12-01 12:42:56,685 - benchmark.runner - INFO - Below is a concise, data-backed roundup of what’s genuinely new in NLP (and closely related multimodal NLP) over the past month (Nov 2025), with specific numbers and verifiable sources.

1) Self-verifiable mathematical reasoning (DeepSeekMath‑V2)
- What’s new: An LLM that couples a verifier + meta‑verifier with a proof generator so the model can identify and fix issues in its own proofs (beyond final‑answer RL).
- Evidence and numbers:
  - Achieves gold-level on IMO 2025 and CMO 2024; 118/120 on Putnam 2024 with scaled test‑time compute (paper).
  - Verifier training data: 17,503 AoPS problems; verifier analyses quality improved from 0.85 → 0.96 under a meta‑verifier measure while maintaining score accuracy.
  - Uses GRPO; verifier rewards combined from format, score alignment, and meta‑verification quality.
- Sources: arXiv: 2511.22570 (pdf: https://arxiv.org/pdf/2511.22570), code: https://github.com/deepseek-ai/DeepSeek-Math-V2

2) Can LMs actually refine their own answers? RefineBench
- What’s new: A checklist‑based, multi‑turn benchmark measuring self‑refinement (no feedback) vs guided refinement (feedback).
- Evidence and numbers:
  - 1,000 problems, 11 domains, 9,898 checklist items (avg 9.9 per instance). Data sources include CA Bar essay, Stanford math/stat, humanities prompts; visual/tabular materials verbalized and validated.
  - Self‑refinement is weak: Gemini 2.5 Pro 31.3%, GPT‑5 29.1%; typical 0–2.6% improvement over 5 turns (e.g., Gemini 2.5 Pro +1.8%; DeepSeek‑R1 −0.1%).
  - Guided refinement is strong: large open‑weights (>70B) and proprietary models approach >90% within five turns.
  - Motivation: 10.24% of 159,134 WildChat queries request refinement.
- Sources: arXiv: 2511.22173 (pdf via HF: https://huggingface.co/papers/2511.22173), dataset: https://huggingface.co/datasets/RefineBench/RefineBench

3) Do captions preserve image utility? CaptionQA
- What’s new: A utility‑based caption evaluation—LLM answers many MCQs using only the caption; compare to answering with the image to quantify the “utility gap.”
- Evidence and numbers:
  - Dataset: 657 images, 33,027 MCQs (50.3/image) across four domains (Natural, Document, E‑commerce, Embodied AI) with a two‑level taxonomy (25 top‑level, 69 subcategories).
  - QA LLM selection (text‑only): Qwen2.5‑72B chosen for faithfulness 92.61%, accuracy 68.98%, ~21.14 QPS; full benchmark ~25 minutes on a single AMD MI325 GPU.
  - Utility gap: Proprietary models show 9.2–16.4% drop from QA‑on‑image to QA‑on‑caption; models scoring similarly on traditional image‑QA can differ by up to 32% on caption utility.
- Sources: arXiv: 2511.21025 (pdf: https://arxiv.org/pdf/2511.21025), dataset: https://huggingface.co/datasets/Borise/CaptionQA, code: https://github.com/bronyayang/CaptionQA

4) Video reasoning that is consistent and grounded (Video‑R2)
- What’s new: Two diagnostics—Think‑Answer Consistency (TAC) and Video Attention Score (VAS)—plus RL with a Temporal Alignment Reward (TAR) to enforce temporally precise, causally coherent reasoning.
- Evidence and numbers:
  - Training data for timestamp‑aware reasoning: 15,271 QA pairs (from ~200K candidates) over 11,816 videos; average duration ~49s; base model Qwen2.5‑VL‑7B.
  - Improves TAC, VAS, and accuracy across 11 video reasoning benchmarks; TAR uses bipartite matching of timestamped claims; reward precision is consistency‑gated.
- Sources: arXiv: 2511.23478 (pdf: https://arxiv.org/pdf/2511.23478)

5) Interactive video reasoning via Chain of Manipulations (Video‑CoM)
- What’s new: “Think with videos,” not just about them—models iteratively manipulate visuals (find‑segment, find‑frame, spatial‑zoom) with step‑level rewards for grounded reasoning.
- Evidence and numbers:
  - Instruction tuning set: 18K examples; RL subset: 3K with dense spatio‑temporal annotations; nine benchmarks.
  - Outperforms recent SOTA by +3.6% average while using ~25K SFT and 3K RL videos.
- Sources: arXiv: 2511.23477 (pdf: https://arxiv.org/pdf/2511.23477), code: https://github.com/mbzuai-oryx/Video-CoM

6) Test‑time learning on open problems (ThetaEvolve)
- What’s new: Open, scalable version of AlphaEvolve with a single LLM, batch sampling, RL at test time, and a large program database; establishes new best‑known bounds using a small open model.
- Evidence and numbers:
  - New bests: Circle packing 2.63598308 (vs 2.63586276 AlphaEvolve; 2.63598283 ShinkaEvolve) and First autocorrelation inequality 1.503133 (vs 1.503164 AlphaEvolve).
  - Speed: circle‑packing program reaches best solution in ~3 s vs ~75 s for ShinkaEvolve.
  - RL at test time consistently beats inference‑only; RL‑trained checkpoints transfer with faster progress, even on unseen tasks.
  - Setup: B=32 parents × n=16 children per step (512 programs/step), response length up to 16,384 tokens; GRPO with asymmetric clipping; 16 evaluators in parallel.
- Sources: arXiv: 2511.23473 (pdf: https://arxiv.org/pdf/2511.23473), code: https://github.com/ypwang61/ThetaEvolve

7) World‑model reasoning for agents with fewer turns (WMAct)
- What’s new: Efficient world‑model internalization via two mechanisms—reward rescaling by action efficacy and interaction frequency annealing—so models solve tasks in a single turn that used to require many.
- Evidence and numbers (single‑turn evaluation on held‑out sets):
  - Success rates: Sokoban 78.57, Maze 88.14, Taxi 62.16.
  - Beats PPO‑EntirePlan (Sokoban 49.12; Maze 75.04; Taxi 45.xx—full table in paper).
  - Curriculum anneals max turns; on‑policy PPO with GAE; 8 samples/prompt; max completion 16k tokens; initial Lmax=30.
- Sources: arXiv: 2511.23476 (pdf: https://arxiv.org/pdf/2511.23476)

8) Visual Document Retrieval (multimodal RAG) for Korean public docs (SDS KoPub VDR)
- What’s new: A large, publicly available VDR benchmark isolating retrieval (not generation) in visually complex Korean government documents—directly relevant to RAG in document intelligence.
- Evidence and numbers:
  - Corpus: 361 documents, 40,781 pages (62.7% pages include visuals). Table‑centric pages: 16,340 (40.1%).
  - Evaluation set: 600 query–page–answer triples; queries by modality: Cross 336, Visual 161, Text 103 (Visual+Cross ≈ 82.8%).
  - Tasks/metrics: text‑only retrieval vs multimodal retrieval; Recall@k and nDCG@k. Multimodal retrieval clearly outperforms text‑only on Visual and Cross queries; substantial gaps remain on cross‑modal reasoning.
- Sources: arXiv: 2511.04910 (pdf via HF: https://huggingface.co/papers/2511.04910), dataset: https://huggingface.co/datasets/SamsungSDS-Research/SDS-KoPub-VDR-Benchmark

9) Systems: training foundation models end‑to‑end on AMD (ZAYA1)
- What’s new: First large‑scale MoE pretraining study entirely on AMD MI300X + Pollara; introduces a small‑MoE model (ZAYA1) with competitive reasoning/coding/math.
- Evidence and numbers:
  - ZAYA1‑base: 760M active params, 8.3B total; reported to outperform Llama‑3‑8B and OLMoE on reasoning, math, and coding benchmarks (paper’s evaluation).
  - Provides Pollara collective microbenchmarks, MI300X‑oriented transformer sizing rules, and fault‑tolerant training recipes.
- Sources: arXiv: 2511.17127 (pdf: https://arxiv.org/pdf/2511.17127), models: https://huggingface.co/Zyphra/ZAYA1-base and https://huggingface.co/Zyphra/ZAYA1-reasoning-base

Additional recent datasets/benchmarks you can use immediately
- CaptionQA (utility‑based caption evaluation): HF dataset: https://huggingface.co/datasets/Borise/CaptionQA (arXiv: 2511.21025)
- RefineBench (LM refinement benchmark): HF dataset: https://huggingface.co/datasets/RefineBench/RefineBench (arXiv: 2511.22173)
- ko‑hallucination benchmark (Korean hallucination eval): n=244 samples across 6 tasks; HF dataset: https://huggingface.co/datasets/jiwon9703/ko-hallucination-benchmark
- SDS KoPub VDR (visual document retrieval for multimodal RAG): https://huggingface.co/datasets/SamsungSDS-Research/SDS-KoPub-VDR-Benchmark (arXiv: 2511.04910)

Takeaways for the month
- Self‑verification is emerging as a credible path to rigorous reasoning (DeepSeekMath‑V2), with strong competition results and concrete verifier training data.
- “Self‑refinement” without guidance remains a weak spot across top models (RefineBench), but providing targeted feedback rapidly closes the gap—important for product design.
- “Utility” is the new metric for captions: even high‑scoring captioners on standard metrics can lose 9–16% utility vs images (and up to 32% between similar models), so evaluate captions in downstream terms (CaptionQA).
- Multimodal reasoning is shifting from passive “think about” to active “think with” videos using interactions, timestamps, and RL rewards grounded in time/space (Video‑R2, Video‑CoM).
- Test‑time RL frameworks (ThetaEvolve) and world‑model RL curricula (WMAct) are showing measurable, transferable gains on hard open problems and interactive tasks—complete with exact score/state‑change metrics.
- For RAG on documents with heavy visual structure, text‑only retrieval underperforms; use multimodal retrieval and measure Recall/nDCG on datasets like SDS KoPub VDR that emphasize visual/cross‑modal queries.

If you need a fast reading/action plan
- Rigorous reasoning: read DeepSeekMath‑V2 (arXiv:2511.22570), try the code; evaluate on your proofs pipeline.
- Model self‑improvement: run RefineBench on your models; measure self‑ vs guided‑refinement differences and iterate your feedback UI accordingly.
- Multimodal and RAG: test CaptionQA to validate caption utility; adopt SDS KoPub VDR to benchmark multimodal retrieval; consider timestamp‑aware or manipulation‑based training if you do video QA.
- Agentic/test‑time learning: try ThetaEvolve for open optimization tasks and WMAct‑style curricula for environment‑based agents.

All items above include concrete stats and direct sources (arXiv IDs, PDFs, and HF links) so you can verify and cite in your presentation.
