1) Video-R2: Reinforcing Consistent and Grounded Reasoning in Multimodal Language Models
- Authors: Muhammad Maaz; Hanoona Rasheed; Fahad Shahbaz Khan; Salman Khan
- Area/date: cs.CV; 2025-11-28
- Links: abs http://arxiv.org/abs/2511.23478v1 | pdf https://arxiv.org/pdf/2511.23478v1
- Problem: MLLMs produce reasoning traces that can be convincing yet logically inconsistent or weakly grounded in visual evidence, especially for dynamic video.
- Diagnostics:
  - TAC (Think Answer Consistency): alignment between reasoning and the final answer.
  - VAS (Video Attention Score): reliance on visual content vs textual priors.
- Findings: Across 11 video reasoning benchmarks, current models rely heavily on linguistic priors over visual evidence.
- Method: A dual post-training stage combining
  - Timestamp-aware supervised fine-tuning (SFT), and
  - GRPO (Group Relative Policy Optimization) with a new Temporal Alignment Reward (TAR) to encourage temporally aligned, causally coherent reasoning.
- Results: The resulting model (Video-R2) improves TAC, VAS, and accuracy consistently across multiple benchmarks, indicating better temporal alignment and reasoning coherence lead to more accurate, trustworthy video understanding.
- Availability: Code, dataset, and model will be open-sourced.

2) Video-CoM: Interactive Video Reasoning via Chain of Manipulations
- Authors: Hanoona Rasheed; Mohammed Zumri; Muhammad Maaz; Ming-Hsuan Yang; Fahad Shahbaz Khan; Salman Khan
- Area/date: cs.CV; 2025-11-28
- Links: abs http://arxiv.org/abs/2511.23477v1 | pdf https://arxiv.org/pdf/2511.23477v1 | code https://github.com/mbzuai-oryx/Video-CoM
- Problem: Most MLLMs “think about videos” only after encoding (purely textual reasoning), creating a semantic bottleneck—no rewatch/refocus/verification—hurting fine-grained spatio-temporal reasoning.
- Paradigm: Interactive Video Reasoning — “think with videos” by turning video into an active cognitive workspace.
- Model: Video-CoM reasons via a Chain of Manipulations (CoM), performing iterative visual actions to gather/refine evidence.
- Training data: Video-CoM Instruct, an 18K instruction-tuning set for multi-step manipulation reasoning.
- Learning: SFT plus RL with reasoning-aware GRPO using step-level reasoning rewards (beyond sparse answer rewards) to enforce grounded, consistent reasoning.
- Results: Strong performance across nine video reasoning benchmarks; +3.6% average over recent SOTA while training on only 25K SFT and 3K GRPO video samples (significantly fewer than comparable large-scale models). Ablations: reasoning-aware rewards improve both accuracy and interpretability.

3) Thinking by Doing: Building Efficient World Model Reasoning in LLMs via Multi-turn Interaction
- Authors: Bao Shu; Yan Cai; Jianjian Sun; Chunrui Han; En Yu; Liang Zhao; Jingcheng Hu; Yinmin Zhang; Haoran Lv; Yuang Peng; Zheng Ge; Xiangyu Zhang; Daxin Jiang; Xiangyu Yue
- Area/date: cs.AI; 2025-11-28
- Links: abs http://arxiv.org/abs/2511.23476v1 | pdf https://arxiv.org/pdf/2511.23476v1
- Problem: LLM agents need robust world-model reasoning; existing multi-turn methods impose rigid reasoning that constrains active learning and efficiency.
- Approach (WMAct): Internalize world models via efficient interaction and active reasoning—letting the model “shape thinking through doing.”
- Key mechanisms:
  - Reward rescaling: adjusts outcome reward by action efficacy to reduce redundancy and incentivize purposeful interaction.
  - Interaction frequency annealing: progressively reduces max interaction turns, forcing condensed learning and internalization (less reliance on environment cues).
- Results: On Sokoban, Maze, and Taxi, WMAct enables solving tasks in a single turn that previously required multiple interactions and shows strong transferability, improving performance on a suite of reasoning benchmarks.

