Tool `Hugging Face:get-paper-info` with Parameter {'arxiv_id': '2511.17127'} on Hugging Face succeeded. Result (summarized from 1467 tokens): - Title: Training Foundation Models on a Full-Stack AMD Platform: Compute, Networking, and System Design
- URL: https://huggingface.co/papers/2511.17127
- Authors: Quentin Anthony; Yury Tokpanov; Skyler Szot; Srivatsan Rajagopal; Praneeth Medepalli; Rishi Iyer; Vasu Shyam; Anna Golubeva; Ansh Chaurasia; Xiao Yang; Tomas Figliolia; Robert Washbourne; Drew Thorstensen; Amartey Pearson; Zack Grossbart; Jason van Patten; Emad Barsoum; Zhenyu Gu; Yao Fu; Beren Millidge

What this work reports
- First large-scale Mixture-of-Experts (MoE) pretraining study conducted entirely on AMD hardware, using MI300X GPUs and the Pollara interconnect.
- End-to-end guidance spanning systems, networking, and model design for AMD-based training at scale.

Systems and networking contributions
- Comprehensive cluster and network characterization on Pollara, including microbenchmarks of all core collectives (all-reduce, reduce-scatter, all-gather, broadcast) across message sizes and GPU counts—claimed as first at this scale.
- MI300X-focused microbenchmarks covering kernel sizing and memory bandwidth to inform efficient model design.

Modeling contributions
- MI300X-aware transformer sizing rules for attention and MLP blocks tailored to AMD hardware characteristics.
- Justification of MoE width choices that jointly optimize training throughput and inference latency.

Training stack and operations
- Detailed description of the training stack, including frequently overlooked utilities:
  - Fault tolerance
  - Checkpoint reshaping
- Training recipe specifics included.

Model preview and performance
- Introduces ZAYA1-base: an MoE with 760M active parameters and 8.3B total parameters.
- ZAYA1-base performance:
  - Comparable to leading base models at its scale and larger (e.g., Qwen3-4B, Gemma3-12B).
  - Outperforms models including Llama-3-8B and OLMoE on reasoning, mathematics, and coding benchmarks.
- Indicates further improvements and follow-up papers are planned.

Key takeaway
- AMD compute, networking (Pollara), and software stack are sufficiently mature and optimized for competitive large-scale pretraining.

