--- Summary of Round 5 ---
Tool `Paper Search:read_arxiv_paper` with Parameter {'paper_id': '2511.22173'} on Paper Search succeeded. Result (summarized from 39190 tokens): Title: RefineBench: Evaluating Refinement Capability of Language Models with Checklists

Goal
Assess whether language models (LMs) can refine their own responses, with or without feedback, across open-ended and verifiable tasks, using a unified, checklist-based multi-turn evaluation.

Key Contributions
- Introduces RefineBench: 1,000 challenging problems across 11 domains with per-problem checklists (avg. 9.9 items) to evaluate refinement.
- Evaluates two modes:
  - Self-refinement: models attempt to improve without guidance.
  - Guided refinement: models receive targeted feedback based on failed checklist items; also supports partially guided refinement with limited feedback.
- Covers both free-form (non-verifiable) and exact-match (verifiable) tasks.
- Provides a controlled, multi-turn protocol to measure progressive improvement and the effect of feedback quantity and specificity.

Motivation and Context
- Real-world interactions often involve refinement; ~10.24% of 159,134 WildChat queries requested refinement.
- Prior work focused mostly on math/code or relied on extrinsic LM-generated critique; generalization to free-form domains was underexplored.
- New reasoning LMs claim self-reflection, warranting re-evaluation.

Benchmark Design
- Data sources: humanities essay prompts (South Korean universities), California Bar Exam law essays, Stanford math/stat problems, and Humanity’s Last Exam (HLE).
- Visual/tabular materials converted to textual descriptions (via GPT-4o/4.1, Claude-Sonnet-3.7) and manually verified.
- Checklist creation: synthesized from problems and reference answers using multiple LMs, then human-refined. Quality control steps:
  - Automatic filtering via backtranslation evaluation (GPT-4.1), removing 1.1% of items.
  - Human validation by PhDs on 100 samples (854 items): 96.1% items judged appropriate.
- Scope and stats:
  - 1,000 instances, 698 passages (avg. 346.96 tokens), 78 materials (46 images verbalized, 32 tables verbalized).
  - 9,898 checklist items; avg./max per instance: 9.9 / 23.
  - 11 domains, 239 subjects; 2 task types (free-form, exact match).
  - Domain distribution: Math 32%, Humanities/Social Sciences 19%, Law 14%.

Evaluation Protocol
- Turns t = 1..5. At each turn:
  1) Refinement: model M generates yt given xt and previous yt−1 (t=1 is initial answer).
  2) Evaluation: evaluator LM (GPT-4.1) judges each checklist item as Yes/No.
  3) Feedback: for guided settings, provide failed checklist items; for partial guidance, provide only a subset N′ = ⌊N × ratio⌋ (“known feedback”) and expect the model to infer the rest.
  - Self-refinement: no explicit feedback.

Metrics
- Acc_t: % of checklist items satisfied at turn t.
- Pass_t: strict pass if all checklist items satisfied (1/0), averaged across instances.

Models Evaluated (34 total)
- Open instruction-tuned: LLaMA-3.1-{8,70,405}B; LLaMA-4-{Scout,Maverick}; Gemma-3-27B; Qwen2.5-72B; Qwen3-30B-A3B-Instruct-2507.
- Proprietary instruction-tuned: GPT-4o-mini, GPT-4o, GPT-4.1; Gemini-2.0-{Flash-Lite, Flash}.
- Open reasoning: DeepSeek-R1 Distill (Qwen {1.5,7,14,32}B; LLaMA-70B); DeepSeek-R1; Qwen3-30B-A3B (thinking); Qwen3-32B (thinking); Qwen3-Next-80B-A3B-Thinking.
- Proprietary reasoning: o1, o3-mini, o4-mini, Grok-3-mini; Claude-Sonnet-3.7/4; Claude-Opus-4/4.1; Gemini-2.5-Flash; GPT-5; Gemini 2.5 Pro.

Main Findings
- Self-refinement is weak:
  - Best self-refinement baselines: Gemini 2.5 Pro 31.3%, GPT-5 29.1%.
